/* ---------- topic pages: places ---------- */
TP({id:'patmos', kind:'Places', title:'Patmos', ch:[1],
 lead:`A small, rocky island in the Aegean Sea where John "was on the island called Patmos because of the word of God and the testimony of Jesus" (1:9). It is the only place in the book that is plainly a real spot on the map where John himself stood.`,
 facts:[['Where','Dodecanese islands, Greece; about 60 miles southwest of Ephesus by sea'],['Size','About 13 square miles; roughly 3,000 people live there today'],['In John’s day','A thinly settled island with a small town, a fort and a shrine of Artemis'],['Known for','The Cave of the Apocalypse and the Monastery of Saint John (UNESCO World Heritage site since 1999)']],
 sections:[
  ['Why was John there?',`Rome had a punishment called <i>relegatio ad insulam</i>, "banishment to an island." It was used for people who were troublesome but not worth executing, often people of some standing. The wording of 1:9, "because of the word of God and my testimony," most naturally means John was sent there for preaching. A few scholars suggest instead that he went there to preach, but the phrase is used elsewhere in Revelation for suffering (6:9; 20:4), and John calls himself a "partner in suffering" in the same verse.`],
  ['What early writers say',`Irenaeus (about AD 180) says the vision was seen "toward the end of Domitian's reign" (<i>Against Heresies</i> 5.30.3), which would be about AD 95. Clement of Alexandria (about 200) says John "returned from the island after the tyrant died" and went back to Ephesus to appoint leaders in the churches. Eusebius (about 325) says the emperor Nerva freed people exiled by Domitian in AD 96 (<i>Church History</i> 3.18–20). Tertullian adds a legend that John was first plunged into boiling oil in Rome and survived. These traditions are early but not certain; see the page on dating.`],
  ['"I was on the island"',`John writes in the past tense ("I was on the island called Patmos"), and some scholars think he wrote the book after he had left. Others think he wrote it there. Either way, the vision came while he was cut off by sea from the seven churches he cared for, which may be part of why the book ends with "the sea is no longer there" (21:1).`],
  ['Patmos today',`The Monastery of Saint John the Theologian was founded in 1088 by the monk Christodoulos, with a grant from the Byzantine emperor Alexios I. Partway down the hill, the Cave of the Apocalypse is shown as the place where John received the vision; a split in the rock ceiling is said to be where the voice came through. Greek Orthodox Christians still keep a festival for John there every year.`]],
 views:{P:`Patmos fixes the book in real history: a Roman exile writing to real churches about events soon to come.`,H:`The starting point of a prophecy that runs through all church history.`,F:`The place where John was taken forward in vision to the end of the age ("Come up here," 4:1).`,I:`A picture of every believer's situation: faithful, isolated, and given a view of what is real.`},
 links:[['Patmos','Patmos'],['Cave of the Apocalypse','Cave of the Apocalypse'],['Monastery of Saint John the Theologian','Monastery of Saint John the Theologian'],['Relegatio','Relegatio']],
 sources:['koester-rev','aune-rev','irenaeus']});

TP({id:'seven-churches', kind:'Places', title:'The Seven Churches of Asia', ch:[1,2,3],
 lead:`Seven real congregations in the Roman province of Asia (western Turkey today), listed in the order a messenger would walk a loop road starting from the port of Ephesus. Each gets a short letter from Jesus in chapters 2–3, and the whole book was sent to all seven (1:11).`,
 sections:[
  {h:'The seven at a glance', table:[['Church','Jesus calls himself','Praised for','Warned about','Promise to the one who wins'],
   ['Ephesus','Holds the seven stars, walks among the lampstands','Hard work, testing false apostles','Losing their first love','Tree of life'],
   ['Smyrna','The First and the Last, who died and came to life','Faithful in poverty and suffering','(nothing)','Crown of life; not hurt by the second death'],
   ['Pergamum','Has the sharp two-edged sword','Holding on where Satan’s throne is','Balaam’s teaching, Nicolaitans','Hidden manna, white stone, new name'],
   ['Thyatira','Son of God, eyes like fire, feet like bronze','Love, faith, service, growth','Tolerating "Jezebel"','Authority over nations, the morning star'],
   ['Sardis','Has the seven Spirits and seven stars','A few with clean clothes','A dead church with a good name','White clothes, name kept in the book of life'],
   ['Philadelphia','Holy, True, holds the key of David','Kept his word with little strength','(nothing)','Pillar in God’s temple, new name'],
   ['Laodicea','The Amen, the faithful and true witness','(nothing)','Lukewarm, proud of wealth','A seat with Jesus on his throne']],
   note:'Two churches get no rebuke (Smyrna and Philadelphia), and they are the two that are poor and pressured. Laodicea, the rich one, gets no praise.'},
  ['Why these seven?',`There were other churches nearby: Colossae, Hierapolis, Troas, Miletus. Seven is Revelation's number of completeness, so seven churches can stand for the whole church. William Ramsay noticed that the seven sit on a circular road, each about 30 to 50 miles from the next, and that each was a center from which a letter could be copied and passed to smaller towns. A messenger landing at Ephesus would visit them in exactly the order of chapters 2–3.`],
  ['The common pattern',`Every letter has the same parts: (1) "To the angel of the church in... write," (2) a title of Jesus taken from the vision in chapter 1, (3) "I know what you do," (4) praise, rebuke, or both, (5) a call to repent or hold on, (6) "Whoever has ears should listen to what the Spirit says to the churches," and (7) a promise "to the one who wins the victory." The promises all point forward to chapters 21–22, where each is fulfilled.`],
  ['Local details',`The letters are full of references to each city's history and geography, which Colin Hemer catalogued in detail: Laodicea's lukewarm water and eye medicine, Sardis's acropolis captured by surprise, Philadelphia's earthquakes, Smyrna's "crown." See each city's page.`],
  {h:'Seven ages of church history?', table:[['Church','Age (a common dispensational scheme)','Approximate dates'],['Ephesus','The apostolic church','AD 30–100'],['Smyrna','The persecuted church','100–313'],['Pergamum','The church joined to the state','313–600'],['Thyatira','The medieval church','600–1517'],['Sardis','The Reformation churches','1517–1750'],['Philadelphia','The missionary church','1750–1900'],['Laodicea','The lukewarm modern church','1900–the end']],
   note:'Popularized by the Scofield Reference Bible (1909) and Clarence Larkin. Dates differ from writer to writer. The text itself addresses the letters to churches existing when John wrote ("I know what you do").'}],
 views:{P:`Seven real first-century churches facing real local pressures, especially the imperial cult and synagogue conflict.`,H:`Seven successive periods of church history, often with Laodicea as the writer's own day.`,F:`Real churches whose letters also preview church history, ending with the lukewarm church before the rapture (many dispensationalists).`,I:`Seven kinds of church that exist in every age; every congregation can find itself in one or more of them.`},
 common:[`Real churches, with lessons for every church ("what the Spirit says to the churches," plural).`],
 uncommon:[`Prophetic ages of church history.`,`Seven types of individual Christian.`],
 links:[['Seven churches of Asia','Seven churches of Asia'],['Roman Asia','Asia (Roman province)']],
 sources:['ramsay-letters','hemer-letters','koester-rev']});

const CITY = (id,name,ch,lead,facts,secs,links,views) => TP({id, kind:'Places', title:name, ch, lead, facts, sections:secs, links, views, sources:['hemer-letters','ramsay-letters','koester-rev']});
CITY('ephesus','Ephesus',[2],`The greatest city of the province, a port of perhaps 200,000 people, home of the temple of Artemis, one of the Seven Wonders of the ancient world. The first church in John's loop and the first to receive a letter (2:1–7).`,
 [['Modern site','Ruins near Selçuk, Turkey, now about 3 miles inland'],['Roman status','Capital of the province of Asia; seat of the governor'],['Famous for','Temple of Artemis; a theater for about 24,000; the temple of the emperors'],['Church history','Paul (Acts 19), Timothy (1 Timothy 1:3), and by tradition John and Mary']],
 [['A city of Artemis and the emperors',`The temple of Artemis was about four times the size of the Parthenon. Its silversmiths rioted when Paul's preaching hurt their trade, shouting "Great is Artemis of the Ephesians!" for two hours (Acts 19:23–41). Under Domitian, Ephesus also built a temple to the Flavian emperors with a colossal statue (its head and arm survive), and proudly took the title <i>neokoros</i>, "temple-keeper" of the emperor.`],
  ['The church',`Paul spent over two years there (Acts 19:10) and warned its elders that "vicious wolves" would come (Acts 20:29–30). The letter praises them for exactly that vigilance: they "put to the test those who call themselves apostles" (2:2). About fifteen years later, Ignatius of Antioch praised Ephesus for the same thing (<i>To the Ephesians</i> 9.1). Their problem was not false teaching but lost love (2:4).`],
  ['"I will remove your lampstand"',`Ephesus fought its river for centuries: the Cayster kept silting up the harbor, and the city had already been moved once. Today the ruins stand several miles from the sea. The church itself flourished for a long time. A great church council met there in 431, and Justinian built a huge basilica over the traditional tomb of John. But by the late Middle Ages the city was abandoned. Many readers see the threat of 2:5 in that history; the text, though, makes it conditional on repentance, not geography.`],
  ['The tree and the paradise',`The promise of "the tree of life, which is in the paradise of God" (2:7) may contrast with Artemis's sanctuary, which, according to tradition, began as a tree shrine and offered asylum to fugitives. Jesus offers a better tree and a true refuge.`]],
 [['Ephesus','Ephesus'],['Temple of Artemis','Temple of Artemis'],['Temple of the Sebastoi','Temple of the Sebastoi'],['Council of Ephesus','Council of Ephesus']],
 {P:`A real church in the 90s AD, doctrinally sound but cooling.`,H:`The apostolic age, to about AD 100.`,F:`The first stage of church history, and a warning to every orthodox church.`,I:`Any church that has the right answers and has lost its love.`});
CITY('smyrna','Smyrna',[2],`Modern Izmir. A rich, beautiful port, fiercely proud of its loyalty to Rome. Its church receives the shortest letter and no rebuke at all (2:8–11).`,
 [['Modern city','Izmir, Turkey, the country’s third-largest city (about 4 million people)'],['Name','<i>Smyrna</i> is the Greek word for myrrh, a perfume used in burials'],['Roman status','Won the right to build a temple to Tiberius in AD 26'],['Famous for','Its beauty, its loyalty to Rome, and as a claimed birthplace of Homer']],
 [['Loyal to Rome',`Smyrna built a temple to the goddess Roma in 195 BC, before Rome was even a great power in the east. In AD 26, eleven cities of Asia competed for the honor of building a temple to the emperor Tiberius; the Roman senate chose Smyrna (Tacitus, <i>Annals</i> 4.55–56). In such a city, refusing to honor the emperor was a public scandal.`],
  ['"Who died and came back to life"',`Old Smyrna was destroyed by the Lydians around 600 BC and lay in villages for about three centuries, until it was refounded on a new site after Alexander. The city had, in a sense, "died and lived again." Jesus introduces himself to them the same way (2:8).`],
  ['"The crown of life"',`The ring of public buildings on Mount Pagos above the city was called "the crown of Smyrna," and crowns (victors' wreaths) were common on its coins and in its festivals. Jesus promises a crown that lasts (2:10).`],
  ['Polycarp',`About sixty years after Revelation, Smyrna's bishop Polycarp, a man who had known John, was arrested and urged to say "Caesar is Lord" and offer incense. He answered, "Eighty-six years I have served him, and he has done me no wrong. How can I blaspheme my King who saved me?" He was burned in the stadium around AD 155 (<i>Martyrdom of Polycarp</i> 8–9). The account says local Jews joined the crowd against him, a sad echo of 2:9.`],
  ['Still standing',`Of the seven cities, Smyrna is the only one that is still a great city today. It had a large Christian population until the burning of 1922.`]],
 [['Smyrna','Smyrna'],['Polycarp','Polycarp'],['Martyrdom of Polycarp','Martyrdom of Polycarp']],
 {P:`A real church facing slander and arrest in the 90s AD.`,H:`The age of Roman persecution (to AD 313); the "ten days" are sometimes read as ten persecutions or Diocletian's ten years.`,F:`The persecuted church in history and today.`,I:`Every church that suffers for its faith and is rich in what matters.`});
CITY('pergamum','Pergamum',[2],`The old royal capital of the region, built on a steep hill a thousand feet above the plain, with a famous library, a great altar of Zeus and a healing center. Its church lives "where Satan's throne is" (2:12–17).`,
 [['Modern site','Bergama, Turkey'],['History','Capital of the Attalid kings; left to Rome by Attalus III in 133 BC'],['Famous for','The Great Altar of Zeus, the library, the Asclepieion (healing shrine), and parchment'],['Imperial cult','First temple to Rome and Augustus in Asia (29 BC)']],
 [['"Where Satan’s throne is"',`Readers have pointed to four candidates. (1) The Great Altar of Zeus, a giant horseshoe-shaped structure on the hilltop, now rebuilt in Berlin's Pergamon Museum. (2) The temple of Rome and Augustus, the first in Asia, which made Pergamum a leader of emperor worship. (3) The sanctuary of Asclepius, the healing god, whose symbol was a snake and who was called "Savior." (4) The Roman court, since the governor held trials there. The most likely meaning is the city's role as a center of emperor worship and Roman power, where refusing it could cost your life (Antipas, 2:13).`],
  ['The library and parchment',`The library at Pergamum was said to hold 200,000 scrolls; Plutarch reports that Mark Antony gave them to Cleopatra for the library of Alexandria. According to Pliny, when Egypt stopped exporting papyrus, Pergamum developed treated animal skins for writing. The word "parchment" comes from the city's name (<i>pergamena</i>).`],
  ['The sword',`Roman governors had the "right of the sword" (<i>ius gladii</i>), the power to execute. Jesus presents himself as the one who really "has the sharp two-edged sword" (2:12, 16).`],
  ['Antipas',`"My faithful witness" (2:13) is the only Christian martyr named in Revelation. Later tradition says he was the city's bishop and was roasted to death in a bronze bull.`],
  ['Hidden manna and the white stone',`Jewish tradition said Jeremiah hid the tent, the ark and the incense altar until God gathers his people again (2 Maccabees 2:4–8). The rabbis added that the jar of manna was hidden away with the ark (Tosefta Sotah 13:1; Talmud, Yoma 52b), and 2 Baruch says the store of manna will come down from on high again in the last days (2 Baruch 29:8). White stones were used as votes for acquittal, as tickets to public banquets, and as amulets with secret names. Jesus offers all three: acquittal, a seat at his feast, and a name of his own giving (2:17).`]],
 [['Pergamon','Pergamon'],['Pergamon Altar','Pergamon Altar'],['Asclepieion of Pergamon','Asclepieion'],['Library of Pergamum','Library of Pergamum']],
 {P:`A real church under pressure from the imperial cult, tempted to compromise at pagan feasts.`,H:`The age of the state church after Constantine (313 to about 600), when "Pergamum" (sometimes taken as "married") joined the world.`,F:`The same church-age reading, plus a warning to churches today.`,I:`Any church living in a place where evil is in power, faithful in crisis but compromising in daily life.`});
CITY('thyatira','Thyatira',[2],`The smallest and least important city of the seven, but the one with the longest letter (2:18–29). A garrison town that became a busy center of trade guilds.`,
 [['Modern site','Akhisar, Turkey'],['History','Founded as a military colony by Seleucus I around 290 BC'],['Famous for','Trade guilds: dyers, wool and linen workers, leather, potters, bronze smiths'],['Chief god','Apollo Tyrimnaios, a sun god, often shown on coins with a double axe']],
 [['A city of guilds',`More trade associations are named in Thyatira's inscriptions than in almost any other city of Asia. Lydia, Paul's first convert in Europe, "sold purple cloth and came from the city of Thyatira" (Acts 16:14). Guild membership meant guild banquets in honor of the gods, which is the setting of the "Jezebel" problem. See the chapter 2 page and the page on Jezebel.`],
  ['"Feet like polished bronze"',`The Greek <i>chalkolibanon</i> (2:18; 1:15) is a rare word found almost nowhere else. Colin Hemer argued it was a local alloy made by Thyatira's bronze workers. Coins of Thyatira show its god as a smith, and Jesus introduces himself as "the Son of God" (the only time in the book), perhaps answering the city's "son of Zeus," Apollo.`],
  ['"Authority over the nations"',`The promise (2:26–27) quotes Psalm 2, a coronation psalm. For working people in a small garrison town who could lose their livelihood by refusing guild feasts, Jesus promises them a share in his rule.`],
  ['Later history',`In the 300s, the writer Epiphanius reported that the church in Thyatira had been taken over by the Montanists, a prophetic movement from Phrygia, and that some critics of Revelation claimed there had never been a church there at all (<i>Panarion</i> 51.33). Akhisar today has almost nothing left of the ancient city.`]],
 [['Thyatira','Thyatira'],['Lydia of Thyatira','Lydia of Thyatira']],
 {P:`A real church divided over guild feasts and a local prophet.`,H:`The medieval church (about 600–1517), with "Jezebel" read as the papacy or the corruption of the church.`,F:`The same church-age reading, with a warning against tolerating false teaching.`,I:`Any church that is loving and active but will not confront what is wrong inside it.`});
CITY('sardis','Sardis',[3],`The old capital of the kingdom of Lydia under King Croesus, famous for wealth, gold and the first coins. By Roman times it was a city living on its past reputation, and its church received the same verdict: "You have a reputation for being alive, but you are dead" (3:1).`,
 [['Modern site','Sart, Turkey'],['History','Capital of Lydia; captured by Cyrus of Persia in 547 BC'],['Famous for','Gold from the Pactolus River; the first true coins (about 600 BC); a huge synagogue'],['Disaster','Destroyed by the earthquake of AD 17 and rebuilt with help from Tiberius']],
 [['"Wake up!"',`Sardis sat on a high, steep acropolis that everyone thought could not be taken. Yet it fell twice by surprise because no one was watching. In 547 BC a Persian soldier saw a defender climb down a crack in the cliff to fetch a dropped helmet, and that night Cyrus's men climbed up the same way (Herodotus 1.84). In 214 BC Antiochus III's men did almost the same thing (Polybius 7.15–18). "If you do not keep watch, I will come like a thief, and you will have no idea at what hour" (3:3).`],
  ['Wealth and the earthquake',`The legendary wealth of Croesus came from gold washed down the Pactolus. In AD 17 a great earthquake struck twelve cities of Asia, and Sardis worst of all. Tiberius gave ten million sesterces and five years free of taxes (Tacitus, <i>Annals</i> 2.47). Sardis rebuilt and named itself in his honor.`],
  ['"Stained clothes"',`Sardis was a center of wool dyeing. The few who "have not stained their clothes" will walk with Jesus "in white" (3:4), the color of victory and purity.`],
  ['The synagogue',`Archaeologists found at Sardis the largest ancient synagogue ever discovered, a remodeled public hall used as a synagogue from about the 200s or 300s AD, right in the city center. Jews were prominent and respected there. Interestingly, the letter to Sardis says nothing about Jewish opposition.`],
  ['Melito',`About eighty years after Revelation, Sardis's bishop Melito wrote a famous Easter sermon and traveled to Israel to learn which books belonged in the Old Testament, the earliest Christian list we have.`]],
 [['Sardis','Sardis'],['Sardis Synagogue','Sardis Synagogue'],['Melito of Sardis','Melito of Sardis']],
 {P:`A real church with a reputation but no life, in a city living on old glory.`,H:`The Reformation churches (about 1517–1750), with a good name but cooling into formality.`,F:`The same church-age reading, and a warning to "nominal" Christianity.`,I:`Any church that looks successful but is spiritually asleep.`});
CITY('philadelphia','Philadelphia',[3],`A younger city on a main road into the interior, "an open door" to the east. Like Smyrna, its church is weak and pressured but receives only praise (3:7–13).`,
 [['Modern site','Alaşehir, Turkey'],['Founded','By the kings of Pergamum in the 100s BC; named for King Attalus II "Philadelphus," "brother-lover"'],['Known as','"Little Athens," for its temples and festivals'],['Disasters','The earthquake of AD 17 and years of aftershocks']],
 [['Earthquakes',`The earthquake of AD 17 hit Philadelphia hard, and aftershocks went on for years. Strabo, writing at the time, says the walls cracked daily and many people lived in the countryside, afraid to stay in the city (<i>Geography</i> 12.8.18; 13.4.10). Jesus promises, "I will make him a pillar in the temple of my God, and he will never go out of it again" (3:12): a building that will never shake, and no more running out of the city.`],
  ['New names',`After the earthquake, the city took the name Neocaesarea ("New Caesar's city") to thank Tiberius, and later Flavia, honoring the emperor Vespasian's family. Its name kept changing with its patrons. Jesus promises names that last: "the name of my God and the name of my God's city... and my new name" (3:12).`],
  ['The open door',`Philadelphia was founded to spread Greek culture inland, a doorway to Phrygia. Jesus holds "the key of David" (Isaiah 22:22) and sets before the church "an open door," one "that no one is able to shut" (Revelation 3:8): perhaps a door for mission, perhaps the door into the kingdom that the "synagogue of Satan" said was closed to them.`],
  ['"An open door that no one can shut"',`Philadelphia remained a Christian Greek city long after the rest of the region fell. It held out, surrounded, until 1390, the last Byzantine city in inner Asia Minor to fall to the Ottomans. Alaşehir still has the standing pillars of a large Byzantine church.`]],
 [['Philadelphia (Alaşehir)','Alaşehir'],['Ignatius, Letter to the Philadelphians','Epistle of Ignatius to the Philadelphians']],
 {P:`A real, small, faithful church in conflict with the local synagogue.`,H:`The missionary and revival age (about 1750–1900), the "open door" of world missions.`,F:`The faithful church that will be kept "from the hour of testing" (3:10), often read as the rapture.`,I:`Any church with little strength and great faithfulness.`});
CITY('laodicea','Laodicea',[3],`A rich banking and textile city in the Lycus Valley, close to Hierapolis and Colossae. The last of the seven and the only one with nothing to praise (3:14–22).`,
 [['Modern site','Ruins near Denizli, Turkey'],['Founded','By the Seleucid king Antiochus II around 260 BC, named for his wife Laodice'],['Famous for','Banking, glossy black wool, and a medical school known for eye ointment'],['Neighbors','Hierapolis (hot springs, 6 miles away) and Colossae (cold water, 10 miles away)']],
 [['"I am rich... and need nothing"',`Laodicea sat where major trade roads crossed and grew wealthy from banking and wool. When the earthquake of AD 60 destroyed it, the city rebuilt with its own money and did not ask Rome for help (Tacitus, <i>Annals</i> 14.27). The Roman statesman Cicero had cashed letters of credit there a century earlier. The church took on the city's self-image (3:17).`],
  ['Gold, white clothes and eye salve',`Jesus's advice (3:18) lists the city's three industries and turns each around. Its banks: "buy from me gold refined in the fire." Its famous black wool: "white clothes to wear." Its medical school, known for a "Phrygian powder" used for eyes: "salve to put on your eyes, so that you can see."`],
  ['Lukewarm water',`Laodicea had no good water of its own. It piped water in through a stone aqueduct from springs several miles away, and the pipes found at the site are thick with mineral deposits. Hierapolis's hot springs were used for healing; Colossae had cold, fresh water. Since a 1958 article by Rudwick and Green, most scholars have read "hot" and "cold" as both good: useful hot water and refreshing cold water. Lukewarm is useless, the kind of water you spit out (3:15–16). Some scholars, Craig Koester among them, doubt that the city's water is behind the image at all: the evidence about the aqueduct is thin, and at ancient meals both hot and cold drinks were served and welcome, so the point may simply be a drink that is neither. The older reading, that "cold" means openly hostile, makes the verse say Jesus would prefer an enemy to a lukewarm friend.`],
  ['Paul’s letter',`Paul mentions a letter to Laodicea that was to be swapped with the Colossians (Colossians 4:16). It has not survived; a later forgery called the "Epistle to the Laodiceans" circulated in the Middle Ages.`],
  ['"I stand at the door and knock"',`The best-known verse in the letters (3:20) is spoken to a church, not to unbelievers: Jesus is outside his own church, asking to come in and eat with whoever opens. Holman Hunt's painting <i>The Light of the World</i> (1853) shows the door with no handle on the outside.`]],
 [['Laodicea on the Lycus','Laodicea on the Lycus'],['Hierapolis','Hierapolis'],['The Light of the World (painting)','The Light of the World (painting)']],
 {P:`A real, wealthy church in the 90s AD, comfortable and complacent.`,H:`The last age of the church, usually the writer's own time.`,F:`The lukewarm church of the last days, often identified with modern Western Christianity.`,I:`Any church that is comfortable, successful and blind to its need.`});

TP({id:'babylon', kind:'Places', title:'Babylon the Great', ch:[14,16,17,18,19],
 lead:`"The great city that holds royal power over the kings of the earth" (17:18), pictured as a prostitute riding the beast. Her fall is announced in 14:8 and 16:19 and mourned in chapter 18. The chapter 18 page has a full study with a map; this page summarizes.`,
 facts:[['Real Babylon','On the Euphrates, about 55 miles south of Baghdad, Iraq'],['Height of power','Nebuchadnezzar II (605–562 BC), who destroyed Jerusalem in 586 BC'],['By John’s day','Mostly ruins; the name stood for proud, God-defying empire'],['Her names in Revelation','Babylon the Great, the great city, the great prostitute, the mother of prostitutes, a mystery, the woman, a queen']],
 sections:[
  ['The historical Babylon',`The empire that destroyed Jerusalem and the first temple in 586 BC and carried Judah into exile. After that, "Babylon" became a byword for proud empire against God (Isaiah 47; Jeremiah 50–51; Daniel 4:30). The prophets' songs over Babylon's fall supply much of Revelation 18's wording.`],
  ['Code for Rome',`Jewish and Christian writers after AD 70 called Rome "Babylon," because Rome too destroyed the temple (1 Peter 5:13; 4 Ezra 3:1–2; 2 Baruch 11:1; Sibylline Oracles 5.143, 159). Rome sat on seven hills (Revelation 17:9) and ruled kings (17:18).`],
  ['Other readings',`<b>Jerusalem</b>, as the unfaithful bride (many preterists, citing 11:8 and 18:24). <b>A rebuilt Babylon</b> in Iraq (some futurists; Saddam Hussein rebuilt parts of it in the 1980s). <b>Papal Rome</b> (the Reformers). <b>A global system</b> of trade and false worship.`],
  ['Her economy',`The cargo list (18:12–13) moves from gold and silk to cattle and chariots, and ends with "slaves, that is, human lives." Babylon falls because its wealth is built on people.`],
  ['The seven kings',`"Five have fallen. One is. The other has not yet come" (17:10). Counting emperors is famously hard: start with Julius or Augustus? Count the three short emperors of AD 69 or skip them? Different counts give different dates for the book. See the page on Rome for a table of the emperors.`]],
 views:{P:`Rome (most) or Jerusalem (some), judged in the first centuries.`,H:`Papal Rome, the standard Protestant reading from the Reformation to the 1800s.`,F:`A future world capital, religious system or rebuilt city in the last days.`,I:`Human society organized against God around power, luxury and false worship, in every age.`},
 common:[`For John's first readers, Rome; for later readers, any power that acts like Rome.`],
 uncommon:[`A literal rebuilt city on the Euphrates.`,`Unfaithful Jerusalem.`],
 claude:{title:'"Come out of her" is about loyalties, not a moving van',body:`"Come out of her, my people" (18:4) quotes Jeremiah 51:45, where Jews in Babylon were told to leave the city. But Revelation's readers lived all over Asia, not in Rome. The reasons given are moral: "so that you have no part in her sins" (18:4). My reading: John is calling Christians to withdraw from Babylon's economy of idolatry and exploitation while living inside it, the way the Laodiceans were told to "buy" from Jesus instead of the banks (3:18). Many scholars read 18:4 this way; the link to the Laodicean letter's market language is my own.`, refs:'Revelation 18:4; Jeremiah 51:45; Revelation 3:17–18; Revelation 18:11–13'},
 links:[['Whore of Babylon','Whore of Babylon'],['Babylon','Babylon'],['Seven hills of Rome','Seven hills of Rome']],
 sources:['bauckham-theology','koester-rev','gregg-four-views']});

TP({id:'rome', kind:'Places', title:'Rome', ch:[13,17,18],
 lead:`Revelation never names Rome, but nearly every scholar agrees it is in view: the beast's power, the seven hills, the city that rules kings, and the sea trade that made it rich. For John's readers, Rome was simply the world.`,
 facts:[['Population','About one million in the city of Rome; perhaps 50–60 million in the empire'],['Ruler in John’s day','Domitian (81–96), if the book dates from the 90s'],['Official religion','The traditional gods, plus honors to Roma and the emperors'],['In Asia','Temples to the emperors in Pergamum, Smyrna and Ephesus']],
 sections:[
  ['The seven hills',`Rome was known everywhere as "the city of seven hills." A coin of Vespasian (AD 71) shows the goddess Roma, armed, seated on seven hills beside the Tiber, with the wolf and twins at her feet. Revelation 17 redraws that coin: a woman seated on seven heads, "seven hills" (17:9), drunk with the blood of God's people.`],
  {h:'The emperors and "five have fallen"', table:[['Emperor','Reign','Notes'],['Augustus','27 BC – AD 14','Jesus born under him (Luke 2:1); called "son of god"'],['Tiberius','14–37','Jesus crucified under him'],['Gaius (Caligula)','37–41','Ordered his statue set up in the Jerusalem temple'],['Claudius','41–54','Expelled Jews from Rome (Acts 18:2)'],['Nero','54–68','First persecution, AD 64; suicide in 68'],['Galba, Otho, Vitellius','68–69','"The year of four emperors"'],['Vespasian','69–79','Crushed the Jewish revolt'],['Titus','79–81','Destroyed Jerusalem and the temple, AD 70'],['Domitian','81–96','Liked to be called "lord and god"'],['Nerva','96–98','Freed exiles, perhaps John']],
   note:'Starting from Augustus and skipping the three short reigns of 68–69 makes Vespasian the sixth ("one is") and Titus the seventh ("for a little while"), which would date the book to the 70s. Starting from Julius Caesar and counting all of them gives Nero or Galba. Many scholars think seven is symbolic and the list cannot be counted.'},
  ['How Rome treated Christians',`At first Christians were seen as a Jewish group. Claudius expelled Jews from Rome around AD 49 after riots "at the instigation of Chrestus," probably a dispute over Christ (Suetonius, <i>Claudius</i> 25.4). Nero blamed Christians for the great fire of AD 64 and had them burned as torches and torn by dogs (Tacitus, <i>Annals</i> 15.44). Under Domitian, there is good evidence of local pressure and some executions, though not an empire-wide campaign. About AD 112, Pliny, governor of a province near Asia, wrote to the emperor Trajan that he executed Christians who refused to offer incense to the emperor's image and curse Christ (<i>Letters</i> 10.96). That letter shows exactly the situation Revelation 13 describes.`],
  ['Peace and prosperity',`Rome's propaganda spoke of the <i>Pax Romana</i>, the Roman peace, and of a golden age of trade. Revelation gives the other side: the red horse's rider is allowed "to take peace away from the earth" (6:4), and the merchants' wealth includes "human lives" (18:13). The Briton Calgacus, in a speech written by Tacitus, said of Rome: "They make a desert and call it peace" (<i>Agricola</i> 30).`]],
 views:{P:`First-century Rome, its emperors and its persecution, judged in the fall of the city or the empire.`,H:`Pagan Rome first, then papal Rome as its successor.`,F:`A revived Roman Empire in the last days, often linked to a ten-nation federation in Europe.`,I:`Rome is the model of every empire that claims divine honors.`},
 links:[['Seven hills of Rome','Seven hills of Rome'],['Roma (personification)','Roma (personification)'],['Domitian','Domitian'],['Persecution of Christians in the Roman Empire','Persecution of Christians in the Roman Empire'],['Pliny the Younger','Pliny the Younger']],
 sources:['koester-rev','bauckham-theology','friesen-cults']});

TP({id:'jerusalem', kind:'Places', title:'Jerusalem in Revelation', ch:[3,11,14,20,21],
 lead:`Two Jerusalems run through the book: the earthly "great city... where their Lord too was crucified" (11:8), and the new Jerusalem coming down from heaven (3:12; 21:2). Readers disagree about how much of the book is about the earthly city.`,
 facts:[['Capital since','About 1000 BC, under David'],['First temple','Built by Solomon; destroyed by Babylon, 586 BC'],['Second temple','Rebuilt 516 BC; expanded by Herod; destroyed by Rome, AD 70'],['After AD 135','Rebuilt by Hadrian as a Roman colony, Aelia Capitolina; Jews barred']],
 sections:[
  ['The city in John’s lifetime',`If John wrote in the 90s, Jerusalem had been in ruins for about twenty-five years. In AD 70, after a long siege, Titus's army burned the temple. Josephus, who was there, describes famine, fire and slaughter, and a huge number of dead (<i>Jewish War</i> 6.420). If John wrote in the late 60s, the siege was about to begin. That one question shapes the whole preterist reading.`],
  {h:'"The great city" in Revelation', table:[['Verse','What it says','Most likely meaning'],['11:8','"where their Lord too was crucified"','Jerusalem, or Rome as the power that crucified him'],['14:8','"Fallen is Babylon the Great"','Babylon'],['16:19','"the great city split into three parts"','Babylon'],['17:18','"the great city that rules over the kings of the earth"','Babylon / Rome'],['18:10, 16, 18, 19, 21','"How terrible for the great city"','Babylon'],['21:10','"the holy city Jerusalem"','The new Jerusalem']],
   note:'Outside 11:8, "the great city" always means Babylon. Preterists who read Babylon as Jerusalem take this as evidence; others think 11:8 means "Babylon" too, crucifying Jesus through Rome’s governor.'},
  ['The beloved city',`"The camp of the saints and the beloved city" (20:9) is surrounded by Gog and Magog at the end of the thousand years. Premillennialists read earthly Jerusalem, the capital of Christ's reign. Amillennialists read the church, as Hebrews speaks of "the city of the living God, heavenly Jerusalem" (Hebrews 12:22).`],
  ['Measured and trampled',`The temple's inner court is measured and protected, but the outer court and "the holy city" are trampled for 42 months (11:1–2), echoing Jesus's words, "Jerusalem will be trampled by Gentiles until the times of the Gentiles are fulfilled" (Luke 21:24).`]],
 views:{P:`Earthly Jerusalem is central: the city judged in AD 70 for rejecting the Messiah.`,H:`Jerusalem mostly as a symbol of the church, true or corrupted.`,F:`Literal Jerusalem in the last days, with a rebuilt temple, then the capital of the millennium.`,I:`The earthly city stands for God's people under trial; the new one for God's people complete.`},
 links:[['Jerusalem','Jerusalem'],['Siege of Jerusalem (70 CE)','Siege of Jerusalem (70 CE)'],['Aelia Capitolina','Aelia Capitolina']],
 sources:['gregg-four-views','koester-rev','josephus']});

TP({id:'new-jerusalem', kind:'Places', title:'The New Jerusalem', ch:[3,21,22],
 lead:`The holy city coming down out of heaven "prepared like a bride" (21:2), a cube about 1,400 miles on each side, with no temple, no sun and no night. The chapter 21 page discusses whether it is literal and what each part may mean.`,
 sections:[
  {h:'The city by the numbers', table:[['Feature','Text','Meaning often seen'],['Shape','A cube: length, width and height equal (21:16)','The Most Holy Place, 20 × 20 × 20 cubits (1 Kings 6:20)'],['Size','12,000 stadia, about 1,400 miles','12 × 1,000: God’s people, complete and vast'],['Wall','144 cubits, about 216 feet (21:17)','12 × 12'],['Gates','12, one pearl each, named for the tribes (21:12, 21)','All Israel; open on every side'],['Foundations','12, named for the apostles (21:14)','The church built on the apostles (Ephesians 2:20)'],['Jewels','12 precious stones (21:19–20)','The high priest’s breastplate (Exodus 28:17–21)'],['Street','Pure gold, like clear glass (21:21)','Even the common ground is holy'],['Light','God’s glory, the Lamb its lamp (21:23)','Isaiah 60:19–20'],['River and tree','From the throne; fruit every month (22:1–2)','Eden restored; Ezekiel 47']]},
  ['Where the picture comes from',`Ezekiel's closing vision is a new temple and city with twelve gates named for the tribes and the name "The LORD is there" (Ezekiel 40–48; Ezekiel 48:30–35). Isaiah promises foundations of lapis lazuli and gates of sparkling stones (Isaiah 54:11–12). Tobit pictures Jerusalem's gates "made of sapphire and emerald" and its streets "set like a mosaic with red gems and with stone of Ophir" (Tobit 13:16–17). Among the Dead Sea Scrolls is the New Jerusalem text, which tours a giant city with measured gates and streets. John gathers all these hopes, then makes one major change: no temple (Revelation 21:22).`],
  ['A city, a bride and a temple',`The city is a bride (21:2, 9–10), a place (21:16), and a sanctuary (21:22). The three pictures belong together: the city is God's people, and it is where God lives with them. "God's tent is with human beings" (21:3).`],
  ['Open gates',`"Its gates will never be shut" (21:25), and "the kings of the earth bring their glory into it" (21:24), quoting Isaiah 60. The kings who earlier made war on the Lamb (19:19) now bring tribute. Some see this as a hint of wide salvation; others as the nations already redeemed.`]],
 views:{P:`The church, or the new covenant order, already begun after AD 70 and to be completed.`,H:`The church's final glory after the last judgment.`,F:`A literal city that will descend to the new earth, possibly hovering over it, home of the redeemed.`,I:`A picture of God's people complete, perfected and living in God's presence.`},
 common:[`A real future home of God's people, described in symbolic language.`],
 uncommon:[`A literal cube 1,400 miles high.`,`The present church only, with no future city.`],
 links:[['New Jerusalem','New Jerusalem'],['Holy of Holies','Holy of Holies'],['New Jerusalem text (Dead Sea Scrolls)','New Jerusalem (Dead Sea Scrolls)']],
 sources:['bauckham-theology','beale-rev','koester-rev']});

TP({id:'mount-zion', kind:'Places', title:'Mount Zion', ch:[14],
 lead:`"I looked, and I saw the Lamb standing on Mount Zion. With him were 144,000" (14:1). The scene answers chapter 13, where the dragon stands on the sand of the sea and the beasts rise.`,
 facts:[['Originally','The fortified hill David captured from the Jebusites (2 Samuel 5:7), the "City of David"'],['Later','The temple mount, and then all Jerusalem'],['Today','The name belongs to a hill southwest of the old city'],['In the Psalms','God’s chosen dwelling, "lovely and lofty" (Psalm 48:2)']],
 sections:[
  ['God’s chosen hill',`Zion is the hill where God placed his king: "I myself have put my king in place on my holy mountain, on Zion" (Psalm 2:6), the same psalm Revelation quotes about the iron rod (Revelation 2:27; 12:5; 19:15). Joel promises, "on Mount Zion and in Jerusalem there will be survivors ... among those who are left are the ones the LORD calls" (Joel 2:32), which may explain the 144,000 standing there as a rescued remnant.`],
  ['Two mountains',`In Jewish writings close to John's time, the Messiah stands on Mount Zion and destroys the gathered nations (4 Ezra 13:35–36). Revelation 14 sets up the opposite picture to chapter 13: the dragon on the sand, the beast rising from the sea, and a crowd marked with its name; then the Lamb on the mountain with a crowd marked with his Father's name.`],
  ['Earthly or heavenly?',`Hebrews says believers "have come near to Mount Zion, to the city of the living God, heavenly Jerusalem" (Hebrews 12:22). Revelation 14:2–3 has the song "from heaven" and "before the throne," which suggests the scene is in heaven. Others read a literal gathering in Jerusalem.`]],
 views:{P:`The faithful Jewish Christians gathered safely, perhaps those who fled Jerusalem before AD 70.`,H:`The true church standing with Christ in every age of persecution.`,F:`Literal Mount Zion in Jerusalem, where Christ will stand with the 144,000 Jewish believers at the start of his reign.`,I:`The heavenly Zion: the redeemed safe with the Lamb, whatever the beast does.`},
 links:[['Mount Zion','Mount Zion'],['City of David','City of David']],
 sources:['beale-rev','koester-rev']});

TP({id:'armageddon', kind:'Places', title:'Armageddon and Megiddo', ch:[16,19],
 lead:`"The kings were brought together to the place called in Hebrew Armageddon" (16:16), from <i>Har Megiddo</i>, "mountain of Megiddo." It is the only place-name in Revelation outside Asia that John explains as Hebrew.`,
 facts:[['Where','Tel Megiddo, northern Israel, guarding a pass into the Jezreel Valley'],['Size of the mound','About 15 acres, with some 20 layers of cities'],['Road','The Via Maris, the main road between Egypt and Mesopotamia'],['Famous battles','Thutmose III (about 1457 BC); Deborah (Judges 5:19); Josiah’s death (609 BC); General Allenby (1918)']],
 sections:[
  {art:'megiddomap', caption:'Megiddo sits where the coastal road crosses the Carmel ridge into the Jezreel Valley, the natural battlefield of the land. The Carmel ridge, where Elijah faced the prophets of Baal, begins just to the northwest, and Nazareth is about 10 miles north across the valley: Jesus grew up looking down on it. Jerusalem is about 55 miles to the south.'},
  ['Megiddo',`A fortress city guarding the pass where the great coastal road crosses into the Jezreel Valley. Whoever held it controlled the trade road between Egypt and Mesopotamia. Pharaoh Thutmose III's battle there (around 1457 BC) is the first battle in history recorded in detail. Deborah and Barak defeated the Canaanite kings "at Taanach, by the waters of Megiddo" (Judges 5:19). King Josiah was killed there trying to stop Pharaoh Necho (2 Kings 23:29), and the mourning for him became proverbial (Zechariah 12:11). In 1918 the British general Allenby defeated the Ottoman army there and took the title "Viscount Allenby of Megiddo."`],
  ['The puzzle: there is no mountain',`Megiddo is a mound on a plain, not a mountain. Suggestions include: (1) the nearby Carmel ridge, where Elijah defeated Baal's prophets (1 Kings 18); (2) a play on <i>har mo'ed</i>, "mount of assembly," where the king of Babylon boasted he would set his throne (Isaiah 14:13); (3) a symbol combining Megiddo's history of battles with the Old Testament picture of nations gathered against God's mountain (Ezekiel 38–39; Joel 3; Zechariah 14). The "Hebrew" note may itself be a clue that the name is meant to be decoded, like the "number of a person" in 13:18.`],
  ['The battle that is never fought',`Revelation gathers the kings for "the battle" three times (16:14; 19:19; 20:8), but never describes fighting. In 19:19–21 the beast and false prophet are captured and the armies killed "by the sword that came out of the rider's mouth." The war is won by a word. The word "Armageddon" now means any huge, final catastrophe, a meaning the text does not quite support.`]],
 views:{P:`A symbolic name for the defeat of Rome's or Jerusalem's forces in the first century.`,H:`A great conflict in history, such as the fall of the Ottoman Empire or a coming war of nations.`,F:`A literal final battle centered in the Jezreel Valley, with armies gathered against Israel before Christ returns.`,I:`The last stand of evil against God, decided by Christ's word, pictured with Israel's most famous battlefield.`},
 links:[['Armageddon','Armageddon'],['Tel Megiddo','Tel Megiddo'],['Battle of Megiddo (15th century BC)','Battle of Megiddo (15th century BC)'],['Jezreel Valley','Jezreel Valley']],
 sources:['koester-rev','aune-rev']});

TP({id:'euphrates', kind:'Places', title:'The Euphrates', ch:[9,16],
 lead:`"The great river," the northern border of the land promised to Abraham (Genesis 15:18) and the eastern edge of the Roman Empire. Twice in Revelation, something terrible comes from across it.`,
 facts:[['Length','About 1,740 miles, from eastern Turkey through Syria and Iraq to the Persian Gulf'],['In Genesis','One of the four rivers of Eden (Genesis 2:14)'],['Cities on it','Babylon, Mari, Carchemish'],['In John’s day','The border between Rome and the Parthian Empire']],
 sections:[
  ['In Revelation',`At the sixth trumpet, four angels "bound at the great river Euphrates" are released with an army of two hundred million cavalry (9:14–16). At the sixth bowl the river dries up "so that the road would be ready for the kings from where the sun rises" (16:12). Both times the river is a dam holding back invasion, and God lets it go.`],
  ['Rome’s nightmare',`The Parthians beyond the Euphrates were the one power Rome never conquered. Their mounted archers destroyed a Roman army at Carrhae in 53 BC and killed the general Crassus, and they defeated another army in AD 62. They were famous for shooting backward while riding away, which may be behind the horses whose "power is in their mouths and in their tails" (9:19). A popular rumor said Nero had not really died but had fled to the Parthians and would return with their armies (Sibylline Oracles 4.119–139).`],
  ['Drying up a river',`Herodotus says Cyrus took Babylon by diverting the Euphrates and marching in along the riverbed (<i>Histories</i> 1.191). Isaiah and Jeremiah speak of God drying up Babylon's waters (Isaiah 44:27; Jeremiah 50:38; Jeremiah 51:36). Drying up water is also how God led Israel out (Exodus 14) and in (Joshua 3). In Revelation 16 the drying is a judgment that opens the road for Babylon's enemies.`],
  ['In the news',`In recent years the Euphrates has dropped sharply because of dams and drought, and some prophecy teachers have linked this to 16:12. Most commentators caution that the text is drawing on the river's history and symbolism, not predicting water levels.`]],
 views:{P:`The Parthian threat, or the armies that came against Jerusalem from the east in AD 66–70.`,H:`The Turks and Islamic armies from the Euphrates region (sixth trumpet), and the weakening of the Ottoman Empire (sixth bowl).`,F:`A literal drying of the river for a vast army from Asia in the last days.`,I:`The boundary that holds back chaos; God can remove it in judgment at any time.`},
 links:[['Euphrates','Euphrates'],['Parthian Empire','Parthian Empire'],['Battle of Carrhae','Battle of Carrhae'],['Nero redivivus legend','Nero redivivus legend']],
 sources:['koester-rev','aune-rev']});

TP({id:'abyss', kind:'Places', title:'The Abyss', ch:[9,11,17,20],
 lead:`<i>Abyssos</i> means "bottomless." In Revelation it is the deep prison of evil: locusts come up from it, the beast rises from it, and the dragon is locked in it for a thousand years.`,
 sections:[
  {h:'The abyss in Revelation', table:[['Verse','What happens'],['9:1–2','A fallen star is given the key to its shaft; smoke rises like a furnace'],['9:3–11','Locusts come out; their king is "the angel of the abyss," Abaddon'],['11:7','The beast from the abyss kills the two witnesses'],['17:8','The beast "is about to come up out of the abyss and go to destruction"'],['20:1–3','An angel with the key binds the dragon and seals him in it']]},
  ['In the Old Testament',`The Greek Old Testament uses <i>abyssos</i> about thirty times, mostly for the Hebrew <i>tehom</i>, the deep waters of creation: "Darkness lay on the face of the deep" (Genesis 1:2). It was the place of chaos God tamed at creation and the depths of the earth (Psalm 71:20). So the "abyss" and the "sea" (13:1) are two ways of saying the same thing: the beast rises from chaos.`],
  ['In Jewish and Christian writings',`In 1 Enoch, fallen angels are bound in a deep place in the earth until judgment (1 Enoch 10:4–6; 18–21). 2 Peter 2:4 and Jude 6 speak of angels held in chains in darkness. The demons in the Gerasene man "kept pleading with him not to order them off into the Abyss" (Luke 8:31). Paul uses the word for the realm of the dead (Romans 10:7).`],
  ['Keys',`The abyss has a key, and it is always given by God (9:1; 20:1). Jesus himself holds "the keys of death and of Hades" (1:18). Evil comes out only when it is let out, and it goes back when it is sent.`],
  {myth:{culture:'Babylon and Greece', title:'Apsu and Tartarus', body:`In Babylonian myth, Apsu was the freshwater deep below the earth, and its partner Tiamat the salt sea, whom Marduk killed to make the world. In Greek myth, Tartarus was a pit as far below Hades as the sky is above the earth, where Zeus locked the defeated Titans. 2 Peter 2:4 uses a form of the word "Tartarus" for where sinful angels are held. The Greek word <i>abyssos</i> is not related to "Apsu," but the picture of a deep prison for defeated rebels was widely shared.`, link:['Tartarus','Tartarus'], link2:'shared picture, no direct borrowing'}}],
 views:{P:`A symbol for the spiritual source of the evil let loose in the first-century world.`,H:`Often linked to the rise of Islam (the smoke and locusts of 9:1–11).`,F:`A literal place where demons are now imprisoned, to be opened in the tribulation and used to hold Satan in the millennium.`,I:`The source of chaos and evil, always under God's key.`},
 links:[['Abyss (religion)','Abyss (religion)'],['Tehom','Tehom'],['Tartarus','Tartarus']],
 sources:['aune-rev','beale-rev']});

TP({id:'temple', kind:'Places', title:'The Temple in Revelation', ch:[3,7,11,14,15,16,21],
 lead:`Revelation is full of temple imagery: lampstands, altar, incense, ark, sea, smoke and priestly clothes. Most of the book takes place in a heavenly temple. Then, at the end, "I did not see a temple in it" (21:22).`,
 sections:[
  {h:'Temple furniture in Revelation', table:[['Object','In Israel’s temple','In Revelation'],['Lampstand','The seven-branched menorah (Exodus 25:31–40)','Seven lampstands = seven churches (1:12, 20)'],['High priest’s robe','Long robe and sash (Exodus 28:4)','Jesus in a long robe and gold sash (1:13)'],['Altar of sacrifice','Blood poured at its base (Leviticus 4:7)','Martyrs’ souls under the altar (6:9)'],['Gold incense altar','Incense before the curtain (Exodus 30:1–10)','Prayers rise with incense (8:3–5)'],['The Sea','Huge bronze basin (1 Kings 7:23)','The sea of glass (4:6; 15:2)'],['Ark of the covenant','In the Most Holy Place','Seen in heaven’s opened temple (11:19)'],['Bowls','Used for offerings (Exodus 25:29)','Bowls of prayers (5:8) and of wrath (16:1)'],['Smoke of glory','Filled Solomon’s temple (1 Kings 8:10–11)','Fills the temple; no one can enter (15:8)'],['Most Holy Place','A perfect cube','The whole city is a cube (21:16)']]},
  ['A heavenly temple',`The Old Testament said Moses built the tabernacle after a pattern shown on the mountain (Exodus 25:40), and Hebrews explains that the earthly sanctuary was only a sketch and a shadow of the heavenly one (Hebrews 8:5). John is shown the reality behind the copy.`],
  ['The measured temple',`In 11:1–2 John measures "the temple of God and the altar" but not the outer court. Readers take this as (1) the real temple before its destruction in AD 70 (preterist), (2) a future rebuilt "third temple" (futurist), or (3) the church, which Paul calls "God's temple" (1 Corinthians 3:16; 2 Corinthians 6:16), protected inwardly while trampled outwardly (many others).`],
  ['No temple',`In the new Jerusalem there is no temple, "because the Lord God, the Almighty, is its temple, and so is the Lamb" (21:22). The temple was always a means to an end: God living among his people. When that happens fully, the building is no longer needed. Jesus had already said, "Tear this temple down, and within three days I will raise it," speaking of his body (John 2:19–21).`],
  {fact:`Today some Jewish and Christian groups prepare for a "Third Temple" in Jerusalem, training priests and making temple vessels. The Temple Mount is also the site of the Dome of the Rock and al-Aqsa Mosque, so the issue is one of the most sensitive in the world.`}],
 views:{P:`The Jerusalem temple before AD 70, and the new temple of the church that replaced it.`,H:`The true church measured and kept, while the outward institutional church is trampled.`,F:`A rebuilt temple in Jerusalem during the tribulation, defiled by the Antichrist (2 Thessalonians 2:4).`,I:`God's people as his dwelling, protected spiritually though persecuted outwardly.`},
 links:[['Third Temple','Third Temple'],['Tabernacle','Tabernacle'],['Holy of Holies','Holy of Holies'],['Second Temple','Second Temple']],
 sources:['beale-rev','bauckham-theology','koester-rev']});

TP({id:'throne', kind:'Symbols', title:'The Throne', ch:[3,4,5,7,12,13,16,20,22],
 lead:`<i>Thronos</i> appears 47 times in Revelation, more than in all the rest of the New Testament together. The book's central question is whose throne is real.`,
 sections:[
  ['God’s throne',`The center of chapter 4. Everything else is placed "around" it: a rainbow, twenty-four thrones, seven lamps, a sea of glass, four living creatures. John never describes the one who sits on it, only light and color: "like jasper and carnelian" (4:3). The picture draws on Isaiah 6, Ezekiel 1 and Daniel 7.`],
  ['Rival thrones',`Satan's throne at Pergamum (2:13), the throne the dragon gives the beast (13:2), and the beast's throne darkened by the fifth bowl (16:10). Every other power in the book is a borrowed or fake throne.`],
  ['The emperor’s court',`David Aune showed that the heavenly throne room looks like Roman imperial ceremony turned upside down. People praised the emperor with shouts of "Worthy!" and hymns; the elders sing "You are worthy, our Lord and God" (4:11), the very title Domitian liked: "our lord and god." Client kings laid their crowns before the emperor; when the Armenian king Tiridates came to Rome he laid his crown before Nero's image (Tacitus, <i>Annals</i> 15.29). The elders "throw their crowns down before the throne" (4:10) of the true Lord.`],
  ['A shared throne',`Jesus sits with his Father on his throne (3:21), and the Lamb stands "in the middle of the throne" (5:6). By the end there is one throne, "the throne of God and of the Lamb" (22:1, 3). And Jesus promises the one who wins a place with him on it (3:21; 20:4).`],
  ['The great white throne',`The last throne in the book is the throne of judgment (20:11), so terrible that earth and sky flee from it. See the page on the great white throne.`]],
 views:{P:`God's rule over Rome and Jerusalem, shown at the moment when he judges them.`,H:`God's rule over all the events of church history.`,F:`The throne room in heaven during the tribulation, before Christ returns to rule on earth.`,I:`The deepest truth of every age: God, not the empire, is on the throne.`},
 links:[['Throne of God','Throne of God'],['Merkabah mysticism','Merkabah mysticism']],
 sources:['bauckham-theology','aune-rev']});

TP({id:'sea', kind:'Symbols', title:'The Sea', ch:[4,13,15,20,21],
 lead:`"And the sea is no longer there" (21:1). <i>Thalassa</i> appears 26 times in Revelation, and it plays several roles, almost all of them dark.`,
 sections:[
  ['Chaos and evil',`The beast rises from the sea (13:1), as Daniel's four beasts did (Daniel 7:2–3). In the Old Testament the sea is the home of Leviathan, the twisting sea monster that God will kill (Isaiah 27:1), and a picture of the nations raging (Isaiah 17:12–13). God's power is shown by his control of the sea (Job 38:8–11; Psalm 89:9; Mark 4:39).`],
  ['Trade and Babylon',`Rome's wealth came by sea. In chapter 18 the captains and sailors stand far off and mourn as they watch Babylon burn (18:17–19).`],
  ['The sea of glass',`Before God's throne is "something like a sea of glass, like crystal" (4:6). Later it is "mixed with fire," and the victors stand beside it singing the song of Moses (15:2–3), like Israel beside the Red Sea after Pharaoh's army drowned.`],
  ['Death',`"The sea gave up the dead who were in it" (20:13). People feared death at sea most, since a drowned body had no grave.`],
  ['Distance',`John was on Patmos, cut off from his churches by the sea. Many preachers have noted that "no more sea" would have meant something personal to him: no more separation.`],
  {myth:{culture:'Canaan and Babylon', title:'Yam, the sea god', body:`At Ugarit, the Canaanite god Baal fights Yam ("Sea") and the seven-headed serpent Litan (Leviathan). In Babylon, Marduk kills Tiamat, the salt sea, and makes the world from her body. Israel's poets borrowed the picture to say the true God rules the sea (Psalm 74:13–14). Revelation goes further: the sea is not just defeated but gone.`, link:['Yam (god)','Yam (god)'], link2:'imagery the Bible deliberately reuses'}}],
 claude:{title:'"No more sea" means no more barrier',body:`Solomon's temple had a huge bronze basin called "the Sea," where priests washed before they served (1 Kings 7:23; 2 Chronicles 4:6). In Revelation 4 the sea of glass stands between the throne and everyone else. In the new creation there is no sea and no temple, and the people see God's face (22:4). My speculation: "no more sea" also means no more barrier and no more need for washing before you may come near, because the washing has already happened ("they washed their robes," 7:14; 22:14).`, refs:'Revelation 21:1; Revelation 4:6; 1 Kings 7:23; 2 Chronicles 4:6; Revelation 7:14; Revelation 22:4, 14'},
 views:{P:`The sea is the Roman world and the Gentile nations; its end is the end of the old order.`,H:`The sea is the nations in turmoil throughout history.`,F:`Some expect a literal earth without oceans; others read "no more sea" as no more chaos.`,I:`The sea is chaos, evil and death, which will be completely gone in the new creation.`},
 links:[['Sea of glass','Sea of glass'],['Molten Sea','Molten Sea'],['Leviathan','Leviathan'],['Chaoskampf','Chaoskampf']],
 sources:['beale-rev','koester-rev','collins-combat']});

/* ---------- topic pages: symbols and numbers ---------- */
TP({id:'sevens', kind:'Symbols', title:'Numbers in Revelation', ch:[1,4,5,6,8,15],
 lead:`Revelation is built on numbers, and most of them carry meaning more than quantity. Ancient readers expected this. Both Greek and Hebrew letters doubled as numerals, and Jewish writers often used numbers as symbols.`,
 sections:[
  {h:'The main numbers', table:[['Number','Usual meaning','Examples'],['Seven','Completeness, from the seven days of creation','Churches, spirits, seals, trumpets, bowls, horns, eyes, heads'],['Four','The whole world','Four corners, four winds, four living creatures'],['Twelve','God’s people','Tribes, apostles, gates, foundations, fruits'],['Three and a half','A limited time of trouble, half of seven','42 months, 1,260 days, "a time, times, and half a time"'],['Ten','A large, round number; earthly power','Ten days (2:10), ten horns, ten crowns'],['A thousand','A huge, full number (10 × 10 × 10)','144,000; 12,000 stadia; the thousand years'],['A third','A large part, but not all','Trumpet judgments'],['Six','One short of seven','666']]},
  ['Seven',`Seven is everywhere: seven churches, spirits, lampstands, stars, seals, horns, eyes, trumpets, thunders, heads, bowls, crowns, kings and mountains. There are also seven "blessed" sayings (1:3; 14:13; 16:15; 19:9; 20:6; 22:7; 22:14). Many scholars find a hidden structure of seven sevens in the whole book.`],
  ['Four',`Four is the world: four corners, four winds, four living creatures. Humanity is described in fourfold lists, "every tribe, language, people, and nation," seven times in the book (5:9; 7:9; 10:11; 11:9; 13:7; 14:6; 17:15). Seven lists of four: the whole world, completely.`],
  ['Twelve',`Twelve tribes and twelve apostles. The 144,000 are 12 × 12 × 1,000; the city is 12,000 stadia with a wall of 144 cubits, twelve gates, twelve foundations and twelve kinds of fruit. Twelve is God's people, multiplied to fullness.`],
  ['Counting words',`Richard Bauckham showed that Revelation also counts key words: "Lamb" for Christ 28 times (7 × 4), "Jesus" 14 times, "Christ" 7 times, "Spirit" 14 times. If he is right, John built the book with the care of a poem.`]],
 claude:{title:'Seven times "the Lord God, the Almighty"',body:`It is tempting to find sevens everywhere, so I checked one that Richard Bauckham points out. The full title <i>kyrios ho theos ho pantokratōr</i>, "the Lord God, the Almighty," occurs exactly seven times in the Greek text (1:8; 4:8; 11:17; 15:3; 16:7; 19:6; 21:22). The word <i>pantokratōr</i> alone occurs nine times: the other two (16:14; 19:15) say "God, the Almighty" without "Lord." So the count holds for the full title, as Bauckham says. The caution is still worth keeping: a count means something only when what is counted is clear, since the same word counted another way gives nine.`, refs:'1:8; 4:8; 11:17; 15:3; 16:7; 19:6; 21:22; 16:14; 19:15'},
 views:{P:`Mostly symbolic numbers, with some literal ones (seven real churches, seven real emperors).`,H:`Time numbers decoded by the day-for-a-year rule: 1,260 days = 1,260 years.`,F:`Many numbers literal: seven years, 144,000 people, 1,000 years, 200 million soldiers.`,I:`All the numbers are symbols of quality, not quantity.`},
 links:[['Biblical numerology','Biblical numerology'],['7 (number)','7 (number)'],['Gematria','Gematria']],
 sources:['bauckham-climax','bauckham-theology']});

TP({id:'three-and-a-half', kind:'Symbols', title:'Three and a Half Years', ch:[11,12,13],
 lead:`One period, named five ways: 42 months (11:2; 13:5), 1,260 days (11:3; 12:6), "a time, and times, and half a time" (12:14), and in miniature, 3½ days (11:9, 11).`,
 sections:[
  {h:'One period, five names', table:[['Name','Verses','Who or what'],['42 months','11:2; 13:5','The holy city trampled; the beast’s authority'],['1,260 days','11:3; 12:6','The two witnesses prophesy; the woman fed in the wilderness'],['A time, times, and half a time','12:14','The woman protected from the serpent'],['3½ days','11:9, 11','The witnesses’ bodies lie in the street']],
   note:'The same span is both the time of the enemy’s power and the time of God’s protection and witness.'},
  ['Where it comes from',`Daniel 7:25 and Daniel 12:7 ("a time, times, and half a time"), and the half-"week" of Daniel 9:27. Elijah's drought, which Luke 4:25 and James 5:17 both say lasted three and a half years. And the Maccabean crisis, when Antiochus IV desecrated the temple from December 167 to December 164 BC, roughly the period Daniel describes.`],
  ['Matching history',`Several events in John's century lasted about this long. Nero's persecution ran from the fire of AD 64 to his death in June 68, and the Jewish war from spring 67 to the fall of Jerusalem in August 70. Preterists point to these.`],
  ['Historicist dates',`Using the day-for-a-year rule, historicists counted 1,260 years. Two favorite reckonings were AD 538 (when the Ostrogoths' siege of Rome failed, which historicists read as freeing the papacy from the Arian Gothic kings) to 1798 (when Napoleon's general took Pope Pius VI prisoner), and 606/607 to 1866/1867. Millerite and Adventist teaching built on such dates.`]],
 claude:{title:'It is Elijah’s number',body:`The Old Testament says Elijah's drought ended "in the third year" (1 Kings 18:1), yet both Jesus (Luke 4:25) and James (James 5:17) call it three and a half years. That means by the first century the drought was remembered as a 3½-year period. Revelation's two witnesses "shut the sky so that no rain falls while they are prophesying" (11:6), for 1,260 days. I think John chose the number partly because it was Elijah's: a time of prophetic witness in a hostile land, with God's people fed in the wilderness (12:6, like Elijah in 1 Kings 17:3–6). Others have noted the Elijah link; tying it to the woman's being "fed" is my addition.`, refs:'1 Kings 18:1; Luke 4:25; James 5:17; Revelation 11:6; Revelation 12:6; 1 Kings 17:3–6'},
 views:{P:`The Roman war of AD 67–70 or Nero's persecution.`,H:`1,260 years by the day-for-a-year rule, often 538–1798.`,F:`Literally 3½ years, the second half (or first half) of a seven-year tribulation.`,I:`The whole church age: a limited, broken time of witness and suffering.`},
 links:[['Time, times and half a time','Time and times and half a time'],['Day-year principle','Day-year principle']],
 sources:['beale-rev','gregg-four-views']});

TP({id:'twelve-tribes', kind:'Symbols', title:'The Twelve Tribes', ch:[7,21],
 lead:`Israel's twelve tribes appear in the sealing of the 144,000 (7:4–8) and on the gates of the new Jerusalem (21:12). John's list is unlike any list in the Old Testament.`,
 sections:[
  {h:'Revelation’s list compared', table:[['Revelation 7:5–8','Genesis 49 (birth order, roughly)','Notes'],['Judah','Reuben','Judah first: the Messiah’s tribe (5:5)'],['Reuben','Simeon','Reuben lost his firstborn place (Genesis 49:4)'],['Gad','Levi','Levi, normally landless, is included in Revelation'],['Asher','Judah',''],['Naphtali','Zebulun',''],['Manasseh','Issachar','A son of Joseph, listed alongside Joseph'],['Simeon','Dan','Dan is missing from Revelation'],['Levi','Gad',''],['Issachar','Asher',''],['Zebulun','Naphtali',''],['Joseph','Joseph','Joseph replaces Ephraim'],['Benjamin','Benjamin','']],
   note:'About twenty different tribal lists appear in the Old Testament, and none matches Revelation’s.'},
  ['Lost tribes?',`Ten northern tribes were scattered by Assyria in 722 BC. In Jesus's day Jews still spoke of twelve tribes (Matthew 19:28; James 1:1; Acts 26:7), expecting God to gather them again (Ezekiel 37:15–28). Revelation pictures that gathering complete.`],
  ['Why no Dan or Ephraim?',`Dan and Ephraim were linked with idolatry: the tribe of Dan set up an idol (Judges 18:30), and King Jeroboam put golden calves at Bethel in Ephraim and at Dan (1 Kings 12:28–30). Irenaeus, about AD 180, said the Antichrist would come from Dan, citing Jeremiah 8:16 ("from Dan comes the sound of his horses snorting") (<i>Against Heresies</i> 5.30.2). Others think the list simply needed exactly twelve once Levi and Joseph were added.`],
  ['Heard and seen',`John "heard how many had been sealed," 144,000 from the tribes (7:4), then "looked, and I saw a crowd so large that no one could count it, from every nation" (7:9). In chapter 5 he heard of a Lion and saw a Lamb. Many readers think the second picture explains the first.`]],
 views:{P:`Jewish Christians of the first century, sealed before Jerusalem's fall.`,H:`The true church, the spiritual Israel.`,F:`144,000 ethnic Jewish believers, 12,000 from each tribe, during the tribulation.`,I:`The whole people of God, pictured as Israel's army, complete and counted.`},
 links:[['Twelve Tribes of Israel','Twelve Tribes of Israel'],['Ten Lost Tribes','Ten Lost Tribes'],['Tribe of Dan','Tribe of Dan']],
 sources:['bauckham-theology','irenaeus']});

TP({id:'lampstands', kind:'Symbols', title:'Lampstands and Stars', ch:[1,2,11],
 lead:`"The seven stars are angels of the seven churches, and the seven lampstands are seven churches" (1:20). Jesus walks among the lampstands and holds the stars in his right hand.`,
 sections:[
  ['Background',`The tabernacle had a single seven-branched gold lampstand, the menorah, which priests tended every evening and morning (Exodus 25:31–40; Exodus 27:20–21). Zechariah saw a lampstand fed by two olive trees, with the message "Not by strength, and not by force, but by my Spirit" (Zechariah 4:1–6). John sees seven separate lampstands: the light that once stood in one sanctuary is now spread among the churches of the world.`],
  ['A lampstand in Rome’s parade',`After Jerusalem fell, the temple's gold menorah was carried through Rome in Titus's victory parade in AD 71. The Arch of Titus, built by Domitian after Titus died in 81, still shows Roman soldiers carrying it. John's readers knew where Israel's lampstand had gone. In Revelation, the risen Jesus walks among lampstands Rome cannot carry off.`],
  ['Removing a lampstand',`Ephesus is warned, "I am coming to you, and I will move your lampstand out of its place, unless you repent" (2:5). A church can stop being a church. The two witnesses are also called "the two olive trees and the two lampstands" (11:4).`],
  {h:'Who are the "angels" of the churches?', table:[['View','Reasoning'],['Heavenly angels','"Angel" means an angel everywhere else in Revelation; Daniel has angels over nations (Daniel 10:20–21)'],['Human leaders or bishops','The word can mean "messenger"; letters were addressed to responsible people'],['The church’s character','The "angel" is the spirit or personality of the church itself, addressed as "you"']]},
  ['Stars',`Ancient people saw stars as heavenly beings (Job 38:7; Judges 5:20). A coin of Domitian's wife shows their dead infant son as a new god, seated on the globe of the world with seven stars around him. Jesus holding seven stars claims the place the emperors' propaganda gave to their own family.`]],
 views:{P:`Seven real churches of the first century, and their leaders or angels.`,H:`The church's light through the ages of history.`,F:`The churches of history, and a warning to churches today.`,I:`Every church is a light-bearer, and Christ is always present among them.`},
 links:[['Menorah (Temple)','Menorah (Temple)'],['Arch of Titus','Arch of Titus']],
 sources:['beale-rev','koester-rev']});

TP({id:'scroll-seals', kind:'Symbols', title:'The Sealed Scroll', ch:[5,6,8],
 lead:`A scroll "with writing on the inside and on the back," sealed with seven seals, in God's right hand (5:1). No one can open it except the Lamb.`,
 sections:[
  ['What kind of document?',`Several kinds of document had this shape. Roman wills were sealed by seven witnesses and could be opened only after the death of the one who made them. Deeds and contracts were written on the inside and summarized on the outside (compare Jeremiah 32:10–14). Ezekiel's scroll was written "on the front and back" with laments (Ezekiel 2:9–10). Daniel was told to seal his book "until the time of the end" (Daniel 12:4).`],
  {h:'The seven seals', table:[['Seal','What happens'],['1','A white horse; the rider goes out to conquer (6:1–2)'],['2','A red horse; peace is taken from the earth (6:3–4)'],['3','A black horse; famine prices (6:5–6)'],['4','A pale horse, Death and Hades; a fourth of the earth (6:7–8)'],['5','Martyrs under the altar cry "How long?" (6:9–11)'],['6','Earthquake, sun black, stars fall; "who can stand?" (6:12–17)'],['(pause)','The 144,000 sealed; the great crowd (7:1–17)'],['7','Silence in heaven, then the seven trumpets (8:1–5)']]},
  ['What does it contain?',`Most readers: God's plan for history and its judgment and redemption. Many futurists: the title deed to the earth, which Christ alone can claim. Some preterists: God's divorce decree against unfaithful Jerusalem. Richard Bauckham: the seals are only being broken in chapters 6–8; the scroll's actual content is opened in chapter 10 and shown from chapter 11 on.`],
  ['Why a will matters',`If the scroll is like a Roman will, it can be opened only by the right heir after a death. The Lamb "who was slaughtered" is "worthy to take the scroll" (5:9). His death makes him able to open it.`]],
 views:{P:`The coming judgment on Jerusalem and the Jewish war, opened in the 60s AD.`,H:`The course of history from the first century, seal by seal.`,F:`The events of the tribulation, beginning after the rapture.`,I:`God's sovereign plan for all history, which only Christ can unfold.`},
 links:[['Seven seals','Seven seals'],['Roman wills','Will and testament#Roman law']],
 sources:['beale-rev','aune-rev','bauckham-climax']});

TP({id:'four-horsemen', kind:'Symbols', title:'The Four Horsemen', ch:[6],
 lead:`White, red, black and pale green horses, released by the first four seals (6:1–8). Each living creature calls "Come!" and a rider goes out with power over part of the earth.`,
 sections:[
  {h:'The four riders', table:[['Horse','Carries','Is given','Most common reading'],['White','A bow, a crown','To conquer','Conquest or war; some say Christ or the gospel'],['Red','A great sword','To take peace from the earth','Civil war and slaughter'],['Black','A pair of scales','Famine prices','Scarcity and economic hardship'],['Pale green','(Death, followed by Hades)','To kill a fourth of the earth','Death by war, famine, disease and wild animals']]},
  ['Background',`Zechariah saw colored horses sent out to patrol the earth for God (Zechariah 1:8–11; Zechariah 6:1–8). Ezekiel names God's "four terrible judgments against Jerusalem, sword and famine and wild animals and plague" (Ezekiel 14:21), the same four as 6:8. Jesus's list of signs is wars, famines, earthquakes and plagues (Matthew 24:6–8; Luke 21:10–11).`],
  ['The white rider',`Irenaeus and many others took him as Christ or the gospel going out, since Christ rides a white horse in 19:11. Others note that this rider has a bow, not a sword from his mouth, and that the next three riders are all disasters; they see a false conqueror or simply military conquest. Rome's feared enemies, the Parthians, were mounted archers known for white horses.`],
  ['Famine prices',`"A quart of wheat for a denarius" (6:6) was about eight to sixteen times the normal price; a laborer's whole day's pay would buy food for one person. "Do not harm the oil and the wine" may reflect a real event: in AD 92 Domitian ordered half the vineyards in the provinces cut down to grow more grain, then backed down after protests from Asia (Suetonius, <i>Domitian</i> 7.2; 14.2).`],
  ['The pale horse',`"Pale" is <i>chlōros</i>, the green-gray color of a corpse (our word "chlorine" comes from it). Death rides it, and Hades, the grave, follows like a wagon picking up bodies.`],
  ['In art',`Albrecht Dürer's 1498 woodcut made the four riders the most famous image in Revelation, and they have appeared in films, novels and music ever since.`]],
 views:{P:`The Roman-Jewish war of AD 66–70, or troubles of the first century.`,H:`Stages of the Roman Empire's history, from prosperity to collapse.`,F:`Judgments at the start of the tribulation, with the white rider often read as the Antichrist.`,I:`The sufferings that run through all history: conquest, violence, scarcity and death, all under God's limit ("a fourth").`},
 links:[['Four Horsemen of the Apocalypse','Four Horsemen of the Apocalypse'],['The Four Horsemen (Dürer)','Four Horsemen (Dürer)']],
 sources:['koester-rev','kovacs-rowland','aune-rev']});

TP({id:'trumpets', kind:'Symbols', title:'The Seven Trumpets', ch:[8,9,10,11],
 lead:`Seven angels blow seven trumpets (8:2–11:19). The first four strike nature, the next two strike people, and the last three are called "woes." Many of them repeat the plagues of Egypt.`,
 sections:[
  {h:'The trumpets and the Exodus plagues', table:[['Trumpet','What happens','Exodus parallel'],['1','Hail and fire mixed with blood; a third of trees burned (8:7)','Plague 7: hail and fire (Exodus 9:23–25)'],['2','A burning mountain thrown into the sea; a third of the sea becomes blood (8:8–9)','Plague 1: water to blood (Exodus 7:20–21)'],['3','A star called Wormwood makes waters bitter (8:10–11)','Plague 1; the bitter water of Marah (Exodus 15:23)'],['4','A third of sun, moon and stars darkened (8:12)','Plague 9: darkness (Exodus 10:21–23)'],['5 (first woe)','Locusts from the abyss torment people for five months (9:1–11)','Plague 8: locusts (Exodus 10:12–15)'],['6 (second woe)','Four angels at the Euphrates; an army of 200 million (9:13–21)','Plague 10: death (Exodus 12:29)'],['7 (third woe)','"The kingdom of the world... has become our Lord\'s and his Messiah\'s" (11:15)','Israel’s song after the Exodus (Exodus 15:18)']]},
  ['What trumpets meant',`Israel's ram's-horn and silver trumpets called the people to battle and to festivals, announced a new king, and warned of attack. At Jericho, seven priests blew seven trumpets for seven days, and the walls fell (Joshua 6). The prophets spoke of "a day of the ram's horn and the battle cry" (Zephaniah 1:16), and Paul of "the last trumpet" at the resurrection (1 Corinthians 15:52). Leviticus 23:24 sets a festival of trumpets.`],
  ['Warning, not the end',`The trumpets hit "a third," never everything. Their purpose is to call people to repent, but "the rest of the human race... did not even repent" (9:20–21). This is why the seventh trumpet is followed by worship, not destruction.`],
  ['The pause',`Between the sixth and seventh trumpets comes a long interlude: the mighty angel and the little scroll (10:1–11) and the two witnesses (11:1–14). The same thing happens between the sixth and seventh seals (chapter 7).`]],
 views:{P:`Events of the Jewish war of AD 66–70.`,H:`The barbarian invasions of the Roman Empire (trumpets 1–4), the rise of Islam (5), and the Ottoman Turks (6). Some 19th-century writers calculated the Ottoman fall for August 11, 1840.`,F:`Literal judgments in the second part of the tribulation.`,I:`God's warnings throughout history, modeled on the plagues of Egypt.`},
 links:[['Seven trumpets','Seven trumpets'],['Shofar','Shofar'],['Plagues of Egypt','Plagues of Egypt']],
 sources:['beale-rev','bauckham-theology','gregg-four-views']});

TP({id:'bowls', kind:'Symbols', title:'The Seven Bowls', ch:[15,16],
 lead:`Seven gold bowls "filled with the anger of God" (15:7), poured out in chapter 16. They repeat the trumpets, but complete: where the trumpets hit a third, the bowls hit everything.`,
 sections:[
  {h:'Trumpets and bowls side by side', table:[['#','Trumpet (8–11)','Bowl (16)'],['1','Earth: hail and fire; a third of trees burned','Earth: painful sores on the beast’s worshipers'],['2','Sea: a third becomes blood','Sea: all becomes blood; everything in it dies'],['3','Rivers: a third made bitter','Rivers and springs become blood'],['4','Sun: a third darkened','Sun: scorches people with fire'],['5','Abyss: locusts torment people','The beast’s throne: his kingdom darkened'],['6','Euphrates: an army released','Euphrates: dried up for the kings of the east; Armageddon'],['7','Loud voices: "The kingdom... has become our Lord’s"','Air: "It is done!" Earthquake, hail, Babylon split']],
   note:'The two series follow the same order, and both echo the plagues of Egypt. Many scholars think they describe the same judgments from two angles, with growing intensity, not two different events.'},
  ['The same bowls',`The gold bowls first appear in 5:8, "gold bowls full of incense. These are the prayers of the saints." The bowls of wrath are handed out from the heavenly temple. Many readers connect them: the prayers of the martyrs, "How long, Master, holy and true? Will you not judge the people who live on the earth" (6:10), are answered in the bowls. The third bowl's angel says, "They poured out the blood of saints and prophets... and you have given them blood to drink" (16:6).`],
  ['No repentance',`Three times the people curse God (16:9, 11, 21), and twice the text adds that they did not repent (16:9, 11). The bowls show that judgment alone does not change hearts.`],
  ['Blessed sayings in the middle',`Right in the middle of the sixth bowl comes a blessing: "Look, I am coming like a thief! Blessed is the one who stays awake" (16:15). Even here, the book speaks to its readers, not just about the world.`]],
 views:{P:`The final judgments on Jerusalem in AD 70 (or on Rome).`,H:`The judgments on the papacy and its supporters from the French Revolution onward.`,F:`The last and most severe judgments at the very end of the tribulation.`,I:`The final, complete judgment of God on a world that refuses him, pictured with Exodus plagues.`},
 links:[['Seven bowls','Seven bowls'],['Plagues of Egypt','Plagues of Egypt']],
 sources:['beale-rev','bauckham-theology']});

TP({id:'seal-of-god', kind:'Symbols', title:'The Seal of God', ch:[7,9,14,22],
 lead:`God's servants are sealed on their foreheads before the winds of judgment blow (7:3). Later they have the Lamb's name and his Father's name written there (14:1; 22:4). The beast's mark (13:16) is a copy of it.`,
 sections:[
  ['What a seal meant',`In the ancient world, a seal pressed into wax or clay showed ownership, protected a document from tampering, and marked it as genuine. Slaves and soldiers were sometimes tattooed or branded with their owner's or general's mark. A seal on the forehead says, "This person belongs to God, and is under his protection."`],
  ['Ezekiel’s mark',`Ezekiel saw a man with a writing kit put a mark on the foreheads of those who grieved over Jerusalem's sins; the executioners were told to spare them (Ezekiel 9:4–6). The mark was the Hebrew letter <i>tav</i>, which in the old script was written like an X or a cross. Early Christians noticed.`],
  ['Passover',`The blood on Israel's doorposts was a sign so that "the destroyer" would pass over (Exodus 12:13, 23). The sealed in Revelation are likewise protected from the plagues (9:4).`],
  ['Protection from what?',`The locusts cannot harm those with the seal (9:4). But sealed people still die as martyrs (6:9–11; 13:7). The seal protects from God's wrath and from losing faith, not from the world's hatred.`],
  ['The seal in the early church',`Paul says believers have been "sealed with the Holy Spirit of promise" (Ephesians 1:13; compare Ephesians 4:30; 2 Corinthians 1:22). By the second century, Christians called baptism "the seal" (2 Clement 7:6; Shepherd of Hermas, <i>Similitudes</i> 9.16).`],
  {fact:`A striking parallel: according to 3 Maccabees 2:29, the Egyptian king Ptolemy IV ordered Jews to be branded with the ivy leaf of Dionysus, the sign of his god, before they could enjoy civic rights. Being marked for a god in order to take part in public life was not an unknown idea.`}],
 views:{P:`The Christians in Judea marked for escape before AD 70.`,H:`The true church, sealed by the Spirit through the ages.`,F:`A real, perhaps visible mark on the 144,000 during the tribulation.`,I:`The Holy Spirit's presence in every believer, marking them as God's own.`},
 links:[['Seal of God','Seal of God'],['Taw','Taw'],['Sealing (Christianity)','Sealing (Christianity)']],
 sources:['beale-rev','bauckham-theology']});

TP({id:'tree-of-life', kind:'Symbols', title:'The Tree of Life', ch:[2,22],
 lead:`Promised to Ephesus (2:7) and standing on both banks of the river in the city (22:2), "producing twelve kinds of fruit," with leaves "for healing the nations." It is the Bible's last picture of Eden.`,
 sections:[
  ['From Eden',`God placed the tree of life in the middle of the garden (Genesis 2:9). After Adam and Eve sinned, God guarded "the way to the tree of life" with cherubim and a flaming sword (Genesis 3:22–24). Ezekiel saw a river from the temple lined with trees bearing fruit every month, whose leaves heal (Ezekiel 47:12). Revelation brings both together.`],
  ['In Jewish writings',`Of wisdom Proverbs says, "To those who grab hold of her she is a tree of life" (Proverbs 3:18). In 1 Enoch 24–25, a fragrant tree near God's throne will be given to the righteous after the judgment. The Testament of Levi says the Messiah "will open the gates of paradise" and give the saints to eat from the tree of life (Testament of Levi 18:10–11).`],
  ['The cross as a tree',`Revelation's word for tree is <i>xylon</i>, which also means "wood." The apostles use the same word for the cross: "the man you put to death by hanging him on a tree" (Acts 5:30; compare 1 Peter 2:24). Early Christian writers and artists loved this connection: the cross is the tree of life.`],
  ['KJV 22:19',`The KJV says "book of life," but the Greek manuscripts say "tree of life." Erasmus had no Greek manuscript of the last verses and translated them back from Latin, where a copying error had changed "tree" (<i>ligno</i>) to "book" (<i>libro</i>).`],
  {myth:{culture:'Ancient Near East', title:'Sacred trees', body:`Assyrian palace carvings show winged spirits tending a stylized "sacred tree," and many cultures told of a world tree or a tree of immortality (the Norse Yggdrasil; the plant of youth in the Epic of Gilgamesh, stolen by a snake). The Ephesian sanctuary of Artemis was also said to have begun at a sacred tree. Genesis and Revelation share the hope of a tree that gives life, but tie it to the one God.`, link:['Tree of life','Tree of life'], link2:'shared hope, different meaning'}}],
 claude:{title:'Eden’s guard becomes twelve angels at open gates',body:`After the fall, God placed cherubim at Eden "to guard the way to the tree of life" (Genesis 3:24). In the new Jerusalem there are angels at the gates again, twelve of them (21:12), but the gates are never shut (21:25), and 22:14 blesses those who "go through the gates" to the tree of life. My speculation: the angels at the gates are Eden's guard turned around, standing beside open doors instead of blocking the way.`, refs:'Genesis 3:24; Revelation 21:12; Revelation 21:25; Revelation 22:14'},
 views:{P:`The life of the new covenant, available now through Christ.`,H:`Eternal life given to the faithful at the end of history.`,F:`A literal tree in the new Jerusalem, perhaps more than one along the river.`,I:`Eternal life and healing, restored to humanity in Christ.`},
 links:[['Tree of life (biblical)','Tree of life (biblical)'],['Garden of Eden','Garden of Eden']],
 sources:['beale-rev','koester-rev']});

TP({id:'ark', kind:'Symbols', title:'The Ark of the Covenant', ch:[11],
 lead:`"The temple of God in heaven was opened, and the ark of his covenant appeared inside his temple" (11:19). The most famous lost object in history appears for one verse.`,
 facts:[['Size','About 45 × 27 × 27 inches (2½ × 1½ × 1½ cubits)'],['Made of','Acacia wood covered with gold, with two gold cherubim on the lid'],['Contents','The stone tablets, a jar of manna, Aaron’s staff (Hebrews 9:4)'],['Last seen','Before Babylon destroyed the temple, 586 BC']],
 sections:[
  ['What happened to it?',`The Bible never says. Theories include: Pharaoh Shishak took it (1 Kings 14:25–26); Babylon took or destroyed it in 586 BC; Jeremiah hid it in a cave on Mount Nebo until God gathers his people (2 Maccabees 2:4–8); an angel hid it in the earth until the end (2 Baruch 6:7–10); or it was taken to Ethiopia, where the Ethiopian Orthodox Church says it is kept at Aksum. Jeremiah himself said a day would come when "people will not say any more, ‘The ark of the covenant of the LORD’... No one will remember it or miss it" (Jeremiah 3:16).`],
  ['Meaning in Revelation',`The ark was the throne of God's presence and the place of atonement. After the seventh trumpet, the hidden presence of God is opened to view, with lightning, thunder and earthquake as at Sinai. Its appearance says God has not forgotten his covenant.`],
  ['The hidden manna',`Rabbinic tradition said the jar of manna was hidden away with the ark (Tosefta Sotah 13:1; Talmud, Yoma 52b), and 2 Baruch says the store of manna will come down from on high again in the last days (2 Baruch 29:8). Jesus's promise of "hidden manna" to Pergamum (2:17) probably draws on that hope.`],
  ['The woman of chapter 12',`The ark appears in 11:19 and the woman "clothed with the sun" in 12:1, with no break in the original. Some Catholic and Orthodox readers link the two and call Mary the "ark of the new covenant," since she carried the Word in her womb (compare Luke 1:35, 39–56 with 2 Samuel 6:2–16). Most Protestant readers see the woman as God's people.`]],
 views:{P:`God's covenant faithfulness revealed as the old temple order ends.`,H:`The restoration of true worship in history.`,F:`Some expect the physical ark to be found and used in a rebuilt temple; others read the heavenly original.`,I:`God's presence and covenant, now open to all.`},
 links:[['Ark of the Covenant','Ark of the Covenant'],['Church of Our Lady Mary of Zion','Church of Our Lady Mary of Zion']],
 sources:['koester-rev','beale-rev']});

TP({id:'little-scroll', kind:'Symbols', title:'The Little Scroll', ch:[10],
 lead:`A mighty angel holds a small open scroll. John is told to take it and eat it: sweet as honey in his mouth, sour in his stomach (10:9–10). Then: "You must prophesy again" (10:11).`,
 sections:[
  ['Background',`Ezekiel was handed a scroll "written on the front and back," told to eat it, and found it "as sweet as honey" before he was sent to a rebellious people (Ezekiel 2:8–3:3). Jeremiah said, "Your words were found, and I ate them. Your word became a joy to me" (Jeremiah 15:16). Eating the scroll means taking God's message completely inside.`],
  ['Sweet and sour',`The word of God is sweet to receive, and bitter to carry, because it contains judgment and suffering, for the world and for the messenger. Chapter 11, which follows immediately, is the story of witnesses who prophesy and are killed.`],
  ['Little scroll or the same scroll?',`John uses a diminutive word, <i>biblaridion</i>, "little scroll." Many think it holds chapter 11 or chapters 11–22. Richard Bauckham argues that it is the same as the sealed scroll of chapter 5, now opened, and that its message is the story of the witnessing church that follows. It is "little" only next to the giant angel.`],
  ['The angel’s oath',`The angel stands on sea and land and swears by the Creator that "there will be no more waiting" (10:6), echoing the angel in Daniel 12:7 who swore it would be "a time, times, and half a time."`]],
 views:{P:`A message about the fate of Jerusalem, sweet in promise and bitter in judgment.`,H:`The Bible rediscovered at the Reformation, sweet to the Reformers but bitter in persecution.`,F:`A further revelation of tribulation events from chapter 11 on.`,I:`The calling of every prophet and preacher: to take God's word in fully and speak it at a cost.`},
 links:[['Revelation 10','Revelation 10']],
 sources:['bauckham-climax','beale-rev']});

TP({id:'seven-thunders', kind:'Symbols', title:'The Seven Thunders', ch:[10],
 lead:`"Seal up what the seven thunders said. Do not write it down" (10:4). It is the only sealed message in a book whose last instruction is "Do not seal up the words" (22:10).`,
 sections:[
  ['Thunder as God’s voice',`Psalm 29 names "the LORD's voice" seven times: "The LORD's voice is over the waters. The God of glory sends out thunder" (Psalm 29:3). When God spoke to Jesus, some in the crowd "said there had been thunder" (John 12:28–29). The seven thunders are a full message from God.`],
  {h:'Thunder in Revelation', table:[['Verse','When it sounds'],['4:5','From the throne, with lightning'],['6:1','The first living creature calls "Come!" with a voice like thunder'],['8:5','Fire from the altar thrown to earth: thunder, lightning, earthquake'],['10:3–4','The seven thunders answer the angel’s roar'],['11:19','The temple opens at the seventh trumpet: thunder, lightning, earthquake, hail'],['14:2','A voice from heaven like thunder, and like harpists playing'],['16:18','The seventh bowl: thunder, lightning, the greatest earthquake'],['19:6','The final "Hallelujah" sounds like mighty thunder']],
   note:'Thunder marks God speaking and each seventh judgment, growing louder each time (8:5; 11:19; 16:18). The only thunder John may not write down is the one in chapter 10.'},
  ['Why seal it?',`Several readings: (1) It was a further cycle of judgments that God canceled, choosing "no more delay" and the call to witness instead (Richard Bauckham). (2) It is a reminder that not everything is revealed: "What is hidden is for the LORD our God" (Deuteronomy 29:29). Paul too heard "unspeakable words, words no human being is allowed to say" (2 Corinthians 12:4). (3) It was meant for John alone.`],
  ['People who claimed to know',`Through the centuries some have claimed to reveal what the thunders said. In the 1960s the American evangelist William Branham taught that their meaning was being revealed in his ministry. Most churches have warned that the text itself tells us not to fill this gap.`]],
 views:{P:`Judgments on Jerusalem that God chose not to reveal.`,H:`Sometimes linked to the "thunders" of the Reformation against Rome.`,F:`Real future judgments that will happen but are not described.`,I:`A lesson in humility: God's plan is bigger than anything written down.`},
 links:[['Seven thunders','Seven thunders'],['Psalm 29','Psalm 29']],
 sources:['bauckham-climax','beale-rev']});

TP({id:'gems', kind:'Symbols', title:'The Twelve Jewels', ch:[21],
 lead:`The twelve foundations of the city wall are decorated with twelve precious stones (21:19–20). Ancient stone names do not always match modern ones, so the identifications below are the most likely, not certain.`,
 sections:[
  {h:'The foundation stones', table:[['#','Greek','Probably','Color'],['1','<i>iaspis</i>','Jasper, or a clear crystal','Clear or green; God’s own color (4:3; 21:11)'],['2','<i>sappheiros</i>','Lapis lazuli','Deep blue with gold flecks'],['3','<i>chalkēdōn</i>','Chalcedony, a kind of agate','Greenish or milky blue'],['4','<i>smaragdos</i>','Emerald','Green'],['5','<i>sardonyx</i>','Sardonyx','Red and white bands'],['6','<i>sardion</i>','Carnelian','Red'],['7','<i>chrysolithos</i>','Gold-colored stone (topaz or yellow jasper)','Yellow'],['8','<i>bēryllos</i>','Beryl','Sea green'],['9','<i>topazion</i>','Peridot','Yellow-green'],['10','<i>chrysoprasos</i>','Chrysoprase','Apple green'],['11','<i>hyakinthos</i>','Sapphire or jacinth','Blue'],['12','<i>amethystos</i>','Amethyst','Purple']],
   note:'Ancient "sapphire" was usually lapis lazuli, and ancient "topaz" was usually peridot, from an island in the Red Sea.'},
  ['The high priest’s breastplate',`Exodus 28:17–21 lists twelve stones set in four rows on the high priest's breastplate, each engraved with the name of a tribe. Eight of Revelation's twelve match the Greek Old Testament's list. In the new Jerusalem, the names of the tribes are on the gates and the names of the apostles on the jeweled foundations (21:12–14): the whole city is dressed like a high priest.`],
  ['Isaiah and Tobit',`"I will set your stones in dark mortar and lay your foundations with lapis lazuli. I will make your battlements of rubies, your gates of sparkling stones" (Isaiah 54:11–12). Tobit 13:16–17 pictures Jerusalem's walls of precious stones. Babylon wears jewels as decoration (17:4); the city of God is built of them.`],
  ['The zodiac?',`The first-century writers Philo and Josephus linked the breastplate's twelve stones with the twelve signs of the zodiac. R. H. Charles noticed that Revelation's order is close to a traditional order of zodiac stones reversed, as if the city undoes the claims of astrology. Others doubt the pattern, since the ancient lists vary.`]],
 views:{P:`A symbol of the glory of the church in the new covenant.`,H:`The church's final glory.`,F:`Literal jeweled foundations in a real city.`,I:`The beauty and value of God's people, the true priesthood.`},
 links:[['Priestly breastplate','Priestly breastplate'],['Lapis lazuli','Lapis lazuli']],
 sources:['aune-rev','koester-rev']});

TP({id:'wormwood', kind:'Symbols', title:'Wormwood', ch:[8],
 lead:`At the third trumpet "a great star, blazing like a torch, fell from the sky" on the rivers and springs. "The name of the star is Wormwood." A third of the waters became bitter, and many people died (8:10–11).`,
 sections:[
  ['The plant',`Wormwood (<i>Artemisia absinthium</i>) is a bitter herb, used in ancient medicine against intestinal worms and later to flavor absinthe. The English name comes from Old English <i>wermōd</i> (compare German <i>Wermut</i>, the source of "vermouth"), not from "worm," though folk etymology linked the two. Its Latin name honors the goddess Artemis, the great goddess of Ephesus. It is bitter but not usually deadly.`],
  ['In the prophets',`Wormwood is the Old Testament's taste of judgment and of sin. "I am about to feed this people wormwood and give them poison water to drink" (Jeremiah 9:15; compare Jeremiah 23:15). "You who turn justice into wormwood" (Amos 5:7). Moses warned against any "root... that bears poison and wormwood," a person who turns away to other gods (Deuteronomy 29:18). Lamentations 3:15, 19 uses it for the bitterness of exile.`],
  ['A falling star',`Stars falling from the sky are signs of judgment (Isaiah 34:4; Mark 13:25), and in Revelation stars can be angels or rulers (1:20; 9:1). Isaiah calls the fallen king of Babylon "Shining One, son of Dawn" (Isaiah 14:12).`],
  ['Chernobyl',`The Ukrainian word <i>chornobyl</i> names mugwort (<i>Artemisia vulgaris</i>), a close relative of wormwood. After the 1986 nuclear disaster, which poisoned the water and land of a huge area, many people connected the two, and some Ukrainian Orthodox writers spoke of it. Most commentators treat the link as a striking coincidence, not a fulfillment.`]],
 views:{P:`Bitter judgment on Jerusalem or the land of Israel during the war of AD 66–70.`,H:`A ruler who devastated the Roman world; many 19th-century historicists named Attila the Hun.`,F:`A literal comet or meteor that poisons the water supply.`,I:`The bitterness that sin and false teaching bring into the world.`},
 links:[['Wormwood (Bible)','Wormwood (Bible)'],['Artemisia absinthium','Artemisia absinthium'],['Chernobyl disaster','Chernobyl disaster']],
 sources:['koester-rev','beale-rev']});

TP({id:'sea-of-glass', kind:'Symbols', title:'The Sea of Glass', ch:[4,15],
 lead:`"Something like a sea of glass, clear as crystal" before the throne (4:6). Later it is "mixed with fire," and those who won the victory over the beast stand beside it with harps and sing the song of Moses (15:2–3).`,
 sections:[
  ['Background',`Under God's feet at Sinai was "what looked like a floor of blue lapis stone, as pure as the sky itself" (Exodus 24:10). Over the living creatures in Ezekiel's vision was "the likeness of a dome, shining like awesome ice" (Ezekiel 1:22). In 1 Enoch 14:9–10, the heavenly house is built of crystal with a floor of crystal. Solomon's temple had a great bronze basin called "the Sea" (1 Kings 7:23).`],
  ['The Red Sea',`In chapter 15, the victors stand beside a sea "mixed with fire" and sing "the song of Moses, the slave of God, and the song of the Lamb" (15:3). The picture is Israel on the far shore of the Red Sea, singing after Pharaoh's army drowned (Exodus 15). The sea that once threatened God's people has become the floor they stand on.`],
  ['Glass in John’s world',`Glassblowing was invented in Syria and Palestine in the first century BC, and by John's day glass was common across the empire. Clear, colorless glass was harder to make and prized. "Glass clear as crystal" was a picture of perfect purity.`],
  {myth:{culture:'Jewish and Islamic tradition', title:'Floors that look like water', body:`The Talmud tells of four rabbis who entered the "garden" of heavenly vision. Rabbi Akiva warned them, "When you reach the stones of pure marble, do not say, ‘Water, water’" (Hagigah 14b). The Qur'an tells how the Queen of Sheba, entering Solomon's palace, thought its floor was a pool of water and lifted her skirts, but it was "a palace paved smooth with glass" (Qur'an 27:44). In all three traditions, a sea of glass is the threshold of a holy king's presence.`, link:['Hekhalot literature','Hekhalot literature'], link2:'shared picture'}}],
 views:{P:`God's throne room as the backdrop to his judgments on the first-century world.`,H:`The calm of God's rule over the troubled nations of history.`,F:`A literal feature of heaven, where tribulation martyrs will stand.`,I:`God's transcendence and holiness, and the victory of his people over chaos.`},
 links:[['Sea of glass','Sea of glass'],['Song of the Sea','Song of the Sea']],
 sources:['beale-rev','koester-rev']});

TP({id:'harvest', kind:'Symbols', title:'The Two Harvests', ch:[14],
 lead:`A grain harvest by "one like a son of man" with a sharp sickle (14:14–16), and a grape harvest thrown into "the great winepress of God's anger" (14:17–20), whose blood flows 1,600 stadia.`,
 sections:[
  ['Two harvests or one?',`Many readers see the grain harvest as the gathering of God's people, since Jesus used harvest for gathering believers (Matthew 13:30; Mark 4:29; John 4:35), and the reaper is "one like a son of man" (1:13). The grape harvest, cut by an angel from the altar, is judgment. Others read both as judgment, following Joel 3:13, which has both pictures in one verse: "Send in the sickle, for the harvest is ripe. Come down and tread, for the winepress is full."`],
  ['The winepress',`Grapes were trampled by foot in a stone basin, and the juice ran out through a channel. Treading grapes was a common picture for judgment: "I have trodden the wine trough by myself... Their juice spattered on my clothes" (Isaiah 63:3). Revelation 19:13–15 picks this up for the returning Christ.`],
  ['"Outside the city"',`The winepress was trodden "outside the city" (14:20). Hebrews says Jesus suffered outside the gate (Hebrews 13:12). Some readers see a hint that the judgment falls where Jesus already suffered.`],
  ['1,600 stadia',`About 184 miles. 1,600 is 4 × 4 × 100, or 40 × 40, suggesting the whole world. Jerome noted that the land of Israel was about 160 Roman miles long, close to the same figure. "As high as horses' bridles" is a picture of overwhelming slaughter found also in 1 Enoch 100:3.`],
  ['"The grapes of wrath"',`Julia Ward Howe's "Battle Hymn of the Republic" (1861) begins, "He is trampling out the vintage where the grapes of wrath are stored," drawing on this chapter and Isaiah 63. John Steinbeck took the title of his 1939 novel from it.`]],
 views:{P:`The judgment of Jerusalem in AD 70, and the gathering of the church.`,H:`Great judgments in history, such as the Reformation and the wars of religion.`,F:`The final harvest at Christ's return: gathering of believers and judgment at Armageddon.`,I:`The end of history: the gathering of God's people and the judgment of evil.`},
 links:[['Grapes of wrath','Grapes of wrath'],['Battle Hymn of the Republic','Battle Hymn of the Republic']],
 sources:['bauckham-theology','beale-rev']});

/* ---------- topic pages: words and texts ---------- */
TP({id:'daniel', kind:'Words', title:'Revelation and Daniel', ch:[1,10,11,12,13,17,20],
 lead:`Revelation borrows from Daniel more than from almost any other book, and it presents itself as Daniel's sealed vision now opened. Daniel 7 in particular is behind chapters 1, 4–5, 13 and 17.`,
 sections:[
  {h:'The main borrowings', table:[['Daniel','Revelation','The picture'],['Daniel 7:13','Revelation 1:7, 13; Revelation 14:14','One like a son of man with the clouds'],['Daniel 7:9','Revelation 1:14','White hair like wool; throne like fire'],['Daniel 7:3–7','Revelation 13:1–2','Beasts from the sea: lion, bear, leopard, ten horns'],['Daniel 7:8, 20','Revelation 13:5','A mouth speaking proud words'],['Daniel 7:10','Revelation 20:12','Books opened at the judgment'],['Daniel 7:24','Revelation 17:12','Ten horns are ten kings'],['Daniel 7:25; Daniel 12:7','Revelation 12:14','"A time, times, and half a time"'],['Daniel 7:21','Revelation 13:7','War on the saints'],['Daniel 7:22, 27','Revelation 20:4','The saints receive the kingdom'],['Daniel 10:5–6','Revelation 1:13–15','A shining figure, eyes like fire, feet like bronze'],['Daniel 12:1','Revelation 12:7','Michael the great prince'],['Daniel 12:4, 9','Revelation 22:10','Sealed, then unsealed'],['Daniel 12:7','Revelation 10:5–6','An angel raises his hand and swears'],['Daniel 2:28–29','Revelation 1:1; Revelation 4:1','"What must happen"']]},
  ['Sealed and unsealed',`Daniel is told to "seal the scroll until the time of the end" (Daniel 12:4, 9). John is told the opposite: "Do not seal up the words of the prophecy of this book, because the time is near" (Revelation 22:10). Revelation presents itself as Daniel opened.`],
  ['How John changes Daniel',`Daniel sees four beasts; John sees one beast made of all four, in reverse order, as if Rome sums up every empire. Daniel's "son of man" comes to the Ancient of Days; in Revelation, the son of man has the Ancient of Days' white hair (1:14), so Jesus shares God's identity. Daniel's saints receive the kingdom; in Revelation they reign because the Lamb has made them a kingdom (1:6; 5:10).`],
  ['When was Daniel written?',`Traditionally, Daniel was written in the 500s BC by Daniel in Babylon. Many modern scholars date its visions to the 160s BC, during Antiochus IV's persecution. Either way, by John's time Daniel was read as a prophecy about the last empire and the coming kingdom, and Jewish writers such as 4 Ezra 12:11–12 openly reinterpreted Daniel's fourth beast as Rome.`]],
 links:[['Book of Daniel','Book of Daniel'],['Daniel 7','Daniel 7'],['Seventy weeks','Prophecy of Seventy Weeks']],
 sources:['beale-rev','koester-rev']});

TP({id:'alpha-omega', kind:'Words', title:'Alpha and Omega', ch:[1,21,22],
 lead:`The first and last letters of the Greek alphabet. God says "I am the Alpha and the Omega" (1:8; 21:6), and Jesus says the same of himself (22:13). It is a way of saying: before everything, after everything, and everything in between.`,
 sections:[
  {h:'Three titles, one meaning', table:[['Title','Spoken by','Verses'],['The Alpha and the Omega','God; then Jesus','1:8; 21:6; 22:13'],['The First and the Last','Jesus','1:17; 2:8; 22:13'],['The Beginning and the End','God; then Jesus','21:6; 22:13']],
   note:'By the last chapter, Jesus claims all three titles together (22:13).'},
  ['Background',`Isaiah 44:6: "I am first, and I am last, and except for me there is no god" (also Isaiah 41:4; Isaiah 48:12). Jewish teachers pointed out that the Hebrew word for truth, <i>emet</i>, uses the first, middle and last letters of the Hebrew alphabet, and called it "the seal of God."`],
  ['Why it matters',`Revelation gives God's own titles to Jesus, one of the New Testament's strongest statements that Jesus shares God's identity. The same God "who is, who was, and who is coming" (1:8) is the Jesus who says "I am coming soon" (22:12–13).`],
  ['In Christian art',`From the 300s on, Christians painted and carved the letters A and Ω beside the cross or the Chi-Rho monogram, on tombs, coins and church walls. They are still used on Easter candles.`],
  {myth:{culture:'Greece', title:'"Zeus was, Zeus is, Zeus will be"', body:`The Greek traveler Pausanias records a chant of the priestesses at Dodona: "Zeus was, Zeus is, Zeus will be" (<i>Description of Greece</i> 10.12.10). Revelation's "who is, who was, and who is coming" (1:4, 8) sounds similar, but notice the change: God is not "who will be" but "who is coming." He does not merely exist in the future; he arrives.`, link:['Dodona','Dodona'], link2:'a parallel John’s readers may have known'}},
  {fact:`A popular modern teaching says the untranslated Hebrew word <i>et</i> (aleph-tav, the first and last letters) in Genesis 1:1 is a hidden name of Jesus. Hebrew grammarians point out that <i>et</i> is an ordinary grammar marker used thousands of times. The real Alpha-and-Omega background is Isaiah.`}],
 links:[['Alpha and Omega','Alpha and Omega'],['Chi Rho','Chi Rho']],
 sources:['bauckham-theology','aune-rev']});

TP({id:'maranatha', kind:'Words', title:'"Come, Lord Jesus"', ch:[22],
 lead:`The book's last prayer, "Amen. Come, Lord Jesus!" (22:20), is the Greek form of an Aramaic prayer of the earliest church: <i>Marana tha</i>, "Our Lord, come!"`,
 sections:[
  ['The Aramaic',`The words can be divided two ways: <i>marana tha</i>, "Our Lord, come!" (a prayer), or <i>maran atha</i>, "Our Lord has come" (a confession). Revelation 22:20 and Didache 10:6 show that the church understood it as a prayer.`],
  ['The oldest Christian prayer?',`Paul leaves it untranslated in a letter to Greek-speaking Corinthians (1 Corinthians 16:22), so it must have come from the very first Aramaic-speaking believers in Jerusalem and been used in worship everywhere. That matters: Aramaic-speaking Jews, within a few years of the resurrection, were already praying to Jesus as "Lord" (<i>mar</i>).`],
  ['At the Lord’s Supper',`The Didache, a church manual from about John's time, ends its communion prayer: "Let grace come and let this world pass away. Hosanna to the God of David. If anyone is holy, let him come; if anyone is not, let him repent. Maranatha. Amen" (Didache 10:6). Revelation ends the same way: an invitation ("let anyone who is thirsty come," 22:17), a warning, and "Come, Lord Jesus."`],
  ['"Anathema Maranatha"',`The KJV prints 1 Corinthians 16:22 as "let him be Anathema Maranatha," and for centuries some readers took "Anathema Maranatha" as a single, extra-strong curse. In fact, "anathema" ends one sentence ("let him be cursed") and "Maranatha" is a separate prayer.`]],
 links:[['Maranatha','Maranatha'],['Didache','Didache']],
 sources:['aune-rev','didache']});

TP({id:'hallelujah', kind:'Words', title:'Hallelujah', ch:[19],
 lead:`Hebrew for "Praise the LORD" (<i>hallelu</i>, "praise," and <i>Yah</i>, short for God's name). Revelation 19:1–6 has it four times, the only uses in the New Testament.`,
 sections:[
  {h:'The four hallelujahs', table:[['Verse','Who sings','Why'],['19:1','A great crowd in heaven','God has judged the great prostitute and avenged his servants'],['19:3','The crowd again','"The smoke from her goes up forever"'],['19:4','The 24 elders and four living creatures','"Amen. Hallelujah!" in worship at the throne'],['19:6','A huge crowd, like rushing waters and thunder','"The Lord our God the Almighty reigns," and the Lamb’s wedding has come']]},
  ['In the Psalms',`It opens and closes many psalms (Psalms 104–106, 111–113, 146–150). Psalms 113–118, called the Hallel, were sung at Passover; Jesus and his disciples sang them after the Last Supper (Mark 14:26).`],
  ['Praise at the fall of the wicked',`The rabbis noticed that the first "Hallelujah" in the book of Psalms comes at the end of Psalm 104, right after "Let sinners be finished off from the earth" (Psalm 104:35). The Talmud says David did not say Hallelujah until he saw the downfall of the wicked (Berakhot 9b–10a). Revelation's first Hallelujahs are also for the fall of Babylon. The praise is not gloating; it is relief that injustice is over.`],
  ['Jerusalem’s streets',`The book of Tobit, written before Revelation, imagines Jerusalem's streets set with gems and says "every house in her will cry, 'Hallelujah!'" (Tobit 13:17–18). Revelation's hallelujahs come right before the bride and the new city.`],
  ['In music',`Handel's "Hallelujah" chorus (1741) sets 19:6, 11:15 and 19:16: "For the Lord God omnipotent reigneth... King of Kings, and Lord of Lords."`]],
 links:[['Hallelujah','Hallelujah'],['Hallel','Hallel'],['Hallelujah (Handel)','Hallelujah (Handel)']],
 sources:['koester-rev','aune-rev']});

TP({id:'word-of-god', kind:'Words', title:'"The Word of God"', ch:[1,6,19,20],
 lead:`The rider on the white horse is named "the Word of God" (19:13), the same title the Gospel of John gives Jesus (John 1:1, 14). In Revelation, God's word is both the message believers die for and the weapon Christ wins with.`,
 sections:[
  ['A sword from the mouth',`Jesus's only weapon is a sword from his mouth (1:16; 2:16; 19:15, 21), from Isaiah 11:4, "the breath of his lips will put the wicked to death," and Isaiah 49:2, "He made my mouth a sharp sword." Hebrews says, "The word of God is alive and at work. It cuts deeper than any sword with two edges" (Hebrews 4:12). Revelation's final battle is won by speech.`],
  ['The warrior Word',`The closest parallel is in the Wisdom of Solomon, written before Revelation, describing the night of the Passover: "Your word, which can do all things, leaped down from heaven, from the royal thrones, a merciless warrior, into the middle of the land marked for ruin, carrying your command, which meant just what it said, like a sharp sword" (Wisdom of Solomon 18:15–16). Revelation 19 takes that picture and gives it a name.`],
  ['"The word of God and the testimony of Jesus"',`This pair runs through the book like a refrain: John writes it (1:2), is exiled for it (1:9), the martyrs die for it (6:9; 20:4), and the woman's children keep it (12:17). "Word of God" is what God has said; "testimony of Jesus" is the witness Jesus gave and his people continue.`],
  ['Words that come true',`"These words are trustworthy and true" (21:5; 22:6). The book ends by warning against adding to or taking away from its words (22:18–19), as Moses warned about the law (Deuteronomy 4:2).`]],
 views:{P:`Christ's word of judgment fulfilled against his enemies in the first century.`,H:`The power of the preached gospel through history.`,F:`Christ's literal return, when his spoken command destroys the armies at Armageddon.`,I:`The truth of God, which defeats every lie in every age.`},
 links:[['Logos (Christianity)','Logos (Christianity)'],['Book of Wisdom','Book of Wisdom']],
 sources:['bauckham-theology','gorman-reading']});

TP({id:'other-traditions', kind:'Words', title:'End-Times Stories from Other Traditions', ch:[6,12,13,19,20,21],
 lead:`Many cultures tell stories about how the world ends and begins again. Some shaped the imagery John used; others were written down much later; some look alike with no known connection. This page gathers them, marked by how likely a real link is.`,
 sections:[
  {h:'At a glance', table:[['Tradition','Story','Revelation parallel','Real link?'],['Babylon, Canaan','A god kills the sea dragon','12; 13; 20','Yes: the Old Testament already uses it'],['Greece','Python hunts pregnant Leto','12','Likely: told at Ephesus'],['Persia','Final savior; dragon chained, then freed','20','Possible, debated'],['Norse','Bound wolf; fire; new green world','20; 21','Unlikely: written down about 1200'],['Hindu','Kalki on a white horse','19:11','No known link'],['Islam','The Dajjal; Jesus returns; Gog and Magog','13; 19; 20','Later; shares biblical roots']]},
  ['The combat myth',`Across the ancient Near East a god defeats a sea dragon: Marduk splits Tiamat (<i>Enuma Elish</i>); Baal crushes Yam and Litan, "the mighty one with seven heads" (Ugarit, about 1300 BC). The Old Testament uses this picture for the true God (Psalm 74:13–14; Isaiah 27:1; Isaiah 51:9). Revelation completes it.`],
  ['Leto and Python',`In the Greek story, the dragon Python hunts the pregnant Leto; she escapes and gives birth to Apollo, who later kills the dragon. Ephesus claimed Leto gave birth near the city (Tacitus, <i>Annals</i> 3.61). Many scholars think Revelation 12 deliberately retells it with Jesus as the true child.`],
  ['Persia',`Zoroastrian teaching expects a final savior (the Saoshyant), resurrection, a river of molten metal that purifies, and the world "made wonderful" (Frashokereti). The dragon Azi Dahaka is chained in a mountain until the end, breaks free, and is destroyed: almost the shape of Revelation 20. Dating the Persian texts is difficult, so scholars debate who influenced whom.`],
  ['Norse',`Fimbulwinter (war and hunger before the end), the wolf Fenrir bound until Ragnarök, fire, and a green world rising from the sea. These were written down by Christians around 1200, so borrowing may run either way.`],
  ['Hindu',`Kalki, the last avatar of Vishnu, rides a white horse with a blazing sword to end the dark age, a striking match to 19:11–15 with no known historical link.`],
  ['Islam',`The Dajjal (the false messiah, with "unbeliever" written on his forehead), the return of Jesus, and Gog and Magog bursting through a wall (Qur'an 18:94–99; 21:96), written down centuries after Revelation.`]],
 claude:{title:'Revelation answers the stories its readers knew',body:`A similar image does not mean John borrowed his faith. Often he takes the most famous story in his readers' world and turns it around: the dragon-slaying god is Jesus, not Apollo; the seven-headed monster is not a cosmic rival but a creature that gets captured; the "goddess Roma" on seven hills is a drunk prostitute. Revelation is as much counter-myth as myth.`, refs:'Revelation 12:1–17; Revelation 13:1; Revelation 17:3–9'},
 links:[['Chaoskampf','Chaoskampf'],['Leto','Leto'],['Frashokereti','Frashokereti'],['Aži Dahāka','Aži Dahāka'],['Ragnarök','Ragnarök'],['Kalki','Kalki'],['Al-Masih ad-Dajjal','Al-Masih ad-Dajjal']],
 sources:['collins-combat','aune-rev']});

TP({id:'translations-guide', kind:'Words', title:'About the Translations', ch:[],
 lead:`The text page beside each chapter can show several translations. Only the one you pick is loaded. Here is what each one is and how they differ.`,
 sections:[
  {h:'The translations compared', table:[['Version','Year','Greek text','Style'],['Plain English','2026','SBLGNT','Meaning for meaning, 8th-grade level'],['KJV','1611 (1769 text)','Textus Receptus','Word for word, Elizabethan English'],['ASV','1901','Westcott-Hort and others','Strictly word for word'],['YLT','1898','Textus Receptus','Extremely literal; keeps Greek tenses'],['WEB','2000s','Majority Text','Modern word for word'],['Greek (SBLGNT)','2010','Critical text','The original language']]},
  ['Plain English',`A fresh rendering made for this site from the Greek (SBLGNT), aiming for about an 8th-grade reading level. It is the only version with notes and "today's words" woven into the text.`],
  ['KJV (King James Version)',`1611, from the Textus Receptus; this is the standard 1769 text. Words the translators added appear in italics, as in printed KJVs. Public domain in the US.`],
  ['WEB (World English Bible)',`A modern public-domain update of the ASV, from the Majority Text.`],
  ['ASV (American Standard Version)',`1901, an American edition of the Revised Version, a careful word-for-word translation. Public domain.`],
  ['YLT (Young’s Literal Translation)',`1898, Robert Young's very literal rendering, which keeps Greek word order and tenses. Public domain.`],
  ['Greek (SBLGNT)',`The SBL Greek New Testament, © 2010 Society of Biblical Literature and Logos Bible Software, used under its free license.`],
  ['Greek word by word',`Each Greek word with its basic meaning and dictionary form, from STEPBible's Translators Amalgamated Greek New Testament (Tyndale House, Cambridge), CC BY 4.0. Words that appear only in the Textus Receptus or Byzantine text (and so in the KJV) are shown faded.`],
  ['Why the Greek texts differ',`Revelation survives in fewer manuscripts than any other New Testament book. When Erasmus printed the first Greek New Testament (1516), he had a single incomplete manuscript of Revelation, and he translated its missing last verses back into Greek from the Latin. His later editions corrected some readings from the Complutensian Polyglot, but others, such as "book of life" in 22:19, stayed and passed through the Textus Receptus into the KJV. See <button class="linkish" data-go="tp-textual-history">How the text came down to us</button>.`],
  ['NIV, ESV and others',`These are copyrighted and cannot be shown in full for a whole book, so each chapter and verse links to Bible Gateway, which opens in a new tab.`]],
 links:[['Bible translations into English','Bible translations into English'],['Textus Receptus','Textus Receptus']],
 sources:['metzger']});

TP({id:'three-angels', kind:'Symbols', title:'The Three Angels of Chapter 14', ch:[14],
 lead:`Three angels fly through the sky with three messages: the eternal good news, the fall of Babylon, and a warning against the beast's mark (14:6–11). Together they are the book's last call before the harvest.`,
 sections:[
  {h:'The three messages', table:[['Angel','Message','Echoes'],['First (14:6–7)','"Fear God and give him glory... worship the one who made heaven and earth"','The fourth commandment (Exodus 20:11); Psalm 96'],['Second (14:8)','"Fallen, fallen is Babylon the Great"','Isaiah 21:9; chapter 18'],['Third (14:9–11)','Anyone who worships the beast "will drink the wine of God’s anger"','Jeremiah 25:15; chapter 16']]},
  ['The "eternal gospel"',`This is the only time Revelation uses the noun "gospel" (<i>euangelion</i>). It is proclaimed to "every nation, tribe, language, and people" (14:6), and its content is a call to worship the Creator. "Fear God and give him glory" is the language of repentance: in 11:13, the survivors of the earthquake "were terrified and gave glory to the God of heaven." It is a last invitation, not only a threat.`],
  ['"The Eternal Gospel" in history',`In the 1200s, the followers of Joachim of Fiore taught that a new age of the Spirit was dawning, and one Franciscan published an "Introduction to the Eternal Gospel" (1254) claiming Joachim's writings would replace the New Testament. The church condemned it.`],
  ['The Adventist reading',`Seventh-day Adventists see their own movement in these three messages, read in a historicist way: the first angel's "the hour of his judgment has come" fulfilled in the Millerite preaching of the 1840s, the second in the call to leave fallen churches, and the third as a warning linked to the Sabbath commandment. Most other traditions read them as a summary of the choice the whole book puts before every reader.`]],
 views:{P:`The gospel preached to the Roman world before Jerusalem's or Rome's fall.`,H:`Specific movements in church history, such as the Reformation or the Adventist movement.`,F:`Literal angels preaching worldwide during the tribulation.`,I:`The church's message in every age: worship God, Babylon will fall, do not take the beast's mark.`},
 links:[['Three Angels’ Messages','Three Angels’ Messages'],['Joachim of Fiore','Joachim of Fiore']],
 sources:['koester-rev','beale-rev']});
